498
00:18:34,067 --> 00:18:37,167
- [Crow] Goodbye, we'll send
you that arm if we find it.

499
00:18:38,900 --> 00:18:41,400
- [Mike] So long you
corn shucking suckers!

500
00:18:43,933 --> 00:18:45,333
- [Tom] A Jam Handy, ha, ha!

501
00:18:47,467 --> 00:18:49,967
(light music)

502
00:18:55,667 --> 00:18:59,300
- [Tom] The story of
Wisconsin sausage.

503
00:18:59,300 --> 00:19:00,400
- [Crow] You get your hand out

504
00:19:00,400 --> 00:19:02,067
of that cookie jar, young man.

505
00:19:03,833 --> 00:19:06,533
- [Mike] Oh he's so lucky,
he has those high cheekbones.

506
00:19:08,400 --> 00:19:10,200
- [Crow] That's the
Edvard Munch painting

507
00:19:10,200 --> 00:19:11,367
that wasn't stolen.

508
00:19:12,900 --> 00:19:14,433
- [Tom] Looks like Ivan Lendel.

509
00:19:16,867 --> 00:19:19,300
- [Crow] No, sorry, can't
work up any interest

510
00:19:19,300 --> 00:19:20,867
in that group of people, sorry.

511
00:19:24,800 --> 00:19:27,200
This isn't a very good
animation sequence.

512
00:19:29,733 --> 00:19:32,567
- [Tom] You know, this is
exactly how Leakey found Lucy.

513
00:19:32,567 --> 00:19:33,400
She was leaky.

514
00:19:35,800 --> 00:19:38,800
- [Crow] This is the best Saul
Bass credit sequence ever.

515
00:19:40,500 --> 00:19:43,600
- [Tom] You know, can't say
these people interest me either.

516
00:19:43,600 --> 00:19:44,933
- [Crow] No, not a lick.

517
00:19:44,933 --> 00:19:46,167
- [Mike] Well how about?

518
00:19:46,167 --> 00:19:47,000
- [Tom] Nah.

519
00:19:47,000 --> 00:19:48,333
- [Crow] No, not a lick.

520
00:19:48,333 --> 00:19:50,067
- [Tom] Well the, no.

521
00:19:50,067 --> 00:19:52,333
- [Crow] Come on, make
with the fright already.

522
00:19:52,333 --> 00:19:54,267
- [Mike] Yeah, bring
on the scare, man.

523
00:19:56,833 --> 00:19:58,300
- [Tom] The big Cunha, Cunha.

524
00:19:59,633 --> 00:20:01,233
- [Mike] Cunha, Cunha

525
00:20:01,233 --> 00:20:03,900
- [Crow] Oh yes, it's
Ralph, it's my picture

526
00:20:03,900 --> 00:20:05,267
we do it my way Brooke.

527
00:20:07,267 --> 00:20:08,700
- [Mike] All right
Brooke, Mr. Auteur,

528
00:20:08,700 --> 00:20:10,600
let's see what you got.

529
00:20:10,600 --> 00:20:12,700
- [Tom] Bring back the
skeleton skull guy.

530
00:20:14,133 --> 00:20:16,033
- [Crow] They are so out
in the middle of the sea.

531
00:20:17,233 --> 00:20:18,900
- [Mike] Fellas, it's
too rough to feed ya.

532
00:20:18,900 --> 00:20:20,500
- Hey, Tony, watch it will ya?

533
00:20:20,500 --> 00:20:22,667
We wanna get back to
port nice and dry.

534
00:20:22,667 --> 00:20:25,000
- Yeah, you trying to
drown us or something?

535
00:20:25,000 --> 00:20:27,700
- Don't worry, just don't worry.

536
00:20:27,700 --> 00:20:28,900
- [Tom] The Exxon Valdez.

537
00:20:30,467 --> 00:20:32,567
- I can navigate this
stuff with one hand.

538
00:20:33,733 --> 00:20:35,600
- Oh Johnny, I think
he's at it again.

539
00:20:36,800 --> 00:20:38,700
- [Mike] Yes, it's
the booze barge.

540
00:20:38,700 --> 00:20:41,067
- [Tom] Save your ID,
you're a fine girl.

541
00:20:42,267 --> 00:20:43,333
- [Crow] Stay right
there I wanna know

542
00:20:43,333 --> 00:20:44,667
where you are at all times.

543
00:20:46,167 --> 00:20:48,467
- If he does that once more
I think I'm gonna get sick.

544
00:20:48,467 --> 00:20:50,433
- I think all this action
has made the fish sick.

545
00:20:50,433 --> 00:20:52,367
I can't seem to make any
contact with 'em at all.

546
00:20:52,367 --> 00:20:53,733
- [Mike] Shut up, wise.

547
00:20:53,733 --> 00:20:55,300
- Okay Betty, let her go.

548
00:20:55,300 --> 00:20:57,033
- [Crow] She's
tossing her panties.

549
00:20:58,200 --> 00:21:00,100
- [Tom] Mm, that's
the last pair, honey.

550
00:21:00,100 --> 00:21:02,467
- Not bad, not bad at all.

551
00:21:02,467 --> 00:21:03,800
- Here, you wanna try it?

552
00:21:03,800 --> 00:21:06,900
- Sure, only there's one hitch.

553
00:21:06,900 --> 00:21:07,733
- What?

554
00:21:07,733 --> 00:21:09,133
- No more bottles.

555
00:21:09,133 --> 00:21:10,367
- [Mike] Well the
captain can rectify that.

556
00:21:10,367 --> 00:21:13,467
- I guess we'll have to
play a different game.

557
00:21:13,467 --> 00:21:14,467
- [Tom] Chutes and
ladders, heh heh.

558
00:21:14,467 --> 00:21:16,500
- What can we do out here?

559
00:21:16,500 --> 00:21:19,200
- Well this is always fun.

560
00:21:19,200 --> 00:21:20,733
- [Crow] Looking at your roots.

561
00:21:23,900 --> 00:21:25,400
(grunting)

562
00:21:25,400 --> 00:21:27,733
Oh Audie I think you really
hurt something this time.

563
00:21:27,733 --> 00:21:29,633
- Of all the girls
in the world I had

564
00:21:29,633 --> 00:21:31,800
to get mixed up with the
daughter of a Judo expert.

565
00:21:31,800 --> 00:21:33,000
- [Tom] Foreshadowing.

566
00:21:33,000 --> 00:21:35,133
- Daddy told me lots
of men like to wrestle.

567
00:21:36,500 --> 00:21:37,700
- Well, that's not exactly
what I had in mind.

568
00:21:37,700 --> 00:21:39,467
- [Crow] I want you
to smash my face

569
00:21:39,467 --> 00:21:40,800
into the side of ship.

570
00:21:43,233 --> 00:21:45,933
- [Mike] I'm glad I
bought my Valero jacket.

571
00:21:45,933 --> 00:21:47,333
- [Tom] How come
you don't Judo me?

572
00:21:47,333 --> 00:21:49,300
- Hey come here, come here.

573
00:21:49,300 --> 00:21:50,467
- What is it?

574
00:21:50,467 --> 00:21:51,800
- [Crow] Water, lots of it.

575
00:21:52,767 --> 00:21:53,933
- There's island way over there

576
00:21:53,933 --> 00:21:55,467
that I don't remember
seeing before.

577
00:21:55,467 --> 00:21:57,833
- [Mike] Yeah, large land
masses are easy to miss.

578
00:21:59,300 --> 00:22:01,700
- Well this is the brightest
day we've had on our vacation.

579
00:22:01,700 --> 00:22:03,500
Musta been hidden in the
mists out there before.

580
00:22:03,500 --> 00:22:06,500
- Oh, a mysterious
hidden island.

581
00:22:06,500 --> 00:22:07,367
Let's go ashore.

582
00:22:07,367 --> 00:22:08,467
- [Tom] Let's go ashore.

583
00:22:08,467 --> 00:22:09,933
- What for?

584
00:22:09,933 --> 00:22:13,067
- Well, maybe we can find
some hidden pirate treasures.

585
00:22:13,067 --> 00:22:15,267
- [Crow] In the gift shop.
- Maybe it would be fun.

586
00:22:15,267 --> 00:22:17,000
Well, what'd ya say, Johnny?

587
00:22:17,000 --> 00:22:18,200
- Oh why not?

588
00:22:18,200 --> 00:22:19,433
This boat's costing us enough.

589
00:22:19,433 --> 00:22:21,167
We might as well see
everything we can.

590
00:22:21,167 --> 00:22:24,500
Hey Tony, put in here,
we wanna go ashore!

591
00:22:24,500 --> 00:22:25,833
- [Mike] Can do!

592
00:22:27,300 --> 00:22:29,367
- [Tom] Robert Maxwell,
the untold story.

593
00:22:31,167 --> 00:22:32,500
- [Crow] I'll just run
up to the lido deck

594
00:22:32,500 --> 00:22:33,900
and see what Issac is doing.

595
00:22:35,900 --> 00:22:38,533
- [Tom] Well he's dead,
why didn't he just say so?

596
00:22:38,533 --> 00:22:40,867
- Boy he's really
tied one on this time.

597
00:22:40,867 --> 00:22:43,667
- Looks like he's gonna
be out for quite awhile.

598
00:22:43,667 --> 00:22:45,167
- What are we gonna do?

599
00:22:45,167 --> 00:22:46,667
- Well, I know I can get
this boat in close enough

600
00:22:46,667 --> 00:22:48,933
to the island to
get anchor down.

601
00:22:48,933 --> 00:22:51,700
But I'm not sure I can get
us back to the main island.

602
00:22:51,700 --> 00:22:53,433
So I guess our little
treasure hunt's

603
00:22:53,433 --> 00:22:55,767
the best thing to do until
our navigator sobers up.

604
00:22:55,767 --> 00:22:58,233
- [Mike] I'll just keep
making wry comments.

605
00:22:58,233 --> 00:22:59,733
- I'll take us in
closer to the shore.

606
00:22:59,733 --> 00:23:00,600
- Okay.

607
00:23:00,600 --> 00:23:01,900
- [Tom] What's a dingy?

608
00:23:01,900 --> 00:23:03,200
- [Crow] Thank
you, please be sure

609
00:23:03,200 --> 00:23:04,733
to fill out your comment cards.

610
00:23:07,333 --> 00:23:09,433
- [Mike] Sure hope you
know how to fly this thing.

611
00:23:10,867 --> 00:23:13,233
- [Crow] Okay, we detonate
at about 100 yards out.

612
00:23:14,733 --> 00:23:18,100
- [Tom] Oh man, I went home
with this boat last night?

613
00:23:18,100 --> 00:23:20,500
- [Mike] Ugh, time to
visit the poop deck

614
00:23:20,500 --> 00:23:22,000
if you know what I mean, geez.

615
00:23:24,633 --> 00:23:27,067
- [Tom] Wait, you forgot
Tallulah Bankhead!

616
00:23:27,067 --> 00:23:29,500
- [Crow] Um, should we
have brought oars, or not?

617
00:23:31,100 --> 00:23:33,167
- [Mike] I could quit anytime.

618
00:23:33,167 --> 00:23:34,400
- Come back!

619
00:23:34,400 --> 00:23:36,267
Come back, you fools!

620
00:23:36,267 --> 00:23:37,700
Don't land there!

621
00:23:38,700 --> 00:23:39,967
Come back!

622
00:23:39,967 --> 00:23:42,500
- [Tom] The cruise
of wine and roses.

623
00:23:43,600 --> 00:23:45,300
- [Crow] All right,
back to work.

624
00:23:45,300 --> 00:23:47,867
(snoring)

625
00:23:47,867 --> 00:23:49,767
- [Mike] Oh no, they
forgot the piano.

626
00:23:51,933 --> 00:23:55,000
- [Crow] Tonight, love and
the landing at Normandy.

627
00:23:56,967 --> 00:23:58,367
- Hey, I wonder if
anyone lives here.

628
00:23:58,367 --> 00:23:59,700
- Oh, no chance.

629
00:23:59,700 --> 00:24:01,033
If there'd have been any
natives on this island

630
00:24:01,033 --> 00:24:02,500
they'd have spotted
the boat before now

631
00:24:02,500 --> 00:24:04,267
been down here trying to sell
us hand painted coconuts.

632
00:24:04,267 --> 00:24:05,533
- What do we do now?

633
00:24:05,533 --> 00:24:07,500
- Well, my intuition tells me

634
00:24:07,500 --> 00:24:10,200
that Captain Kidd buried
his loot over there.

635
00:24:10,200 --> 00:24:11,900
- [Crow] In her bra?
- Hey, let's race up there.

636
00:24:11,900 --> 00:24:14,100
Last one doesn't get
any of the jewels, huh?

637
00:24:16,767 --> 00:24:19,833
- [Tom] Hey, counter worker
from Long John Silver's.

638
00:24:19,833 --> 00:24:20,767
- [Mike] Go fish.

639
00:24:20,767 --> 00:24:21,733
- [Crow] Shoot, I wanted to sell

640
00:24:21,733 --> 00:24:23,867
them painted coconuts, arr.

641
00:24:27,400 --> 00:24:29,500
- See what'd I tell ya. This
looks just like the remains

642
00:24:29,500 --> 00:24:31,300
of Captain Morgan's flag ship.

643
00:24:31,300 --> 00:24:33,500
- But back there you
said it was Captain Kidd.

644
00:24:33,500 --> 00:24:34,700
- I did?

645
00:24:34,700 --> 00:24:35,967
- [Tom] So you're saying
I'm not good in bed?

646
00:24:35,967 --> 00:24:37,800
- One pirate's gold is
as good as another's.

647
00:24:37,800 --> 00:24:38,967
- [Mike] Back pedaling.

648
00:24:40,933 --> 00:24:42,167
- I think I found something.

649
00:24:42,167 --> 00:24:44,267
- [Crow] Oh, my hand, sorry.

650
00:24:44,267 --> 00:24:45,433
- Oh what is it?

651
00:24:46,400 --> 00:24:48,133
Armor, pirate's armor?

652
00:24:48,133 --> 00:24:51,500
- Well, let's just say it's
something the tide washed in.

653
00:24:51,500 --> 00:24:53,700
- [Tom] Remember, I
know more than you do.

654
00:24:53,700 --> 00:24:56,333
- I think you're just a little
too young to know about it.

655
00:24:56,333 --> 00:24:58,167
- [Mike] Yeah, women know
nothing about underwear.

656
00:24:58,167 --> 00:24:59,300
- Hey gang, come here.

657
00:24:59,300 --> 00:25:00,533
- [Tom] I'm Robert Reed.

658
00:25:04,333 --> 00:25:06,967
Found some turtle
eggs let's smash 'em.

659
00:25:06,967 --> 00:25:09,133
- [Mike] The head, I think.

660
00:25:09,133 --> 00:25:10,233
- Did you find
something, Johnny?

661
00:25:10,233 --> 00:25:11,533
- You bet I did, look.

662
00:25:11,533 --> 00:25:12,600
- [Tom] Linguini.

663
00:25:12,600 --> 00:25:13,867
- Ew, what is it?

664
00:25:13,867 --> 00:25:15,100
- It's some of the best
eating in these waters.

665
00:25:15,100 --> 00:25:17,000
A lot of clams around here, too.

666
00:25:17,000 --> 00:25:17,933
- I'm getting hungry.

667
00:25:17,933 --> 00:25:19,533
Hey, let's have a clam bake.

668
00:25:19,533 --> 00:25:21,500
- Right, let's have
it tropical style.

669
00:25:21,500 --> 00:25:22,767
- Well how do you mean?

670
00:25:22,767 --> 00:25:23,700
- Well look, Pete and I
will go into the jungle

671
00:25:23,700 --> 00:25:24,933
and get some banana leaves.

672
00:25:24,933 --> 00:25:27,267
- [Mike] In the back,
it's a Japanese sub.

673
00:25:27,267 --> 00:25:29,067
Hey, Robert, Japanese sub.

674
00:25:29,067 --> 00:25:31,300
- That's fine, all
except the last part.

675
00:25:31,300 --> 00:25:32,233
- Huh?

676
00:25:32,233 --> 00:25:34,467
- Where you go, we go, right?

677
00:25:34,467 --> 00:25:35,900
- Right.

678
00:25:35,900 --> 00:25:37,900
- We'll all go for the leaves
and then we'll all dig.

679
00:25:37,900 --> 00:25:39,100
- Okay, okay.

680
00:25:39,100 --> 00:25:40,600
Come on, let's go.

681
00:25:40,600 --> 00:25:44,000
- [Crow] Unfortunately they
landed on Bikini Atoll in 1947.

682
00:25:45,633 --> 00:25:47,300
- [Mike] The lure of
the clam bake proved

683
00:25:47,300 --> 00:25:49,100
to be their undoing.

684
00:25:49,100 --> 00:25:51,667
- [Tom] Great plan, look, can
we just go to Red Lobster?

685
00:25:51,667 --> 00:25:53,500
- What is a jungle
without banana trees?

686
00:25:53,500 --> 00:25:54,667
- [Crow] Uh, a forest.

687
00:25:54,667 --> 00:25:55,667
- Hey wait a minute.

688
00:25:57,067 --> 00:25:59,800
- [Crow] If you lived
here you'd home now, what?

689
00:25:59,800 --> 00:26:02,500
- It's not too old, either. I
can still smell the tobacco.

690
00:26:02,500 --> 00:26:04,367
- That means that
somebody does live here.

691
00:26:04,367 --> 00:26:05,833
- Well, not necessarily.

692
00:26:05,833 --> 00:26:07,000
Maybe somebody just went
exploring a couple days ago

693
00:26:07,000 --> 00:26:08,767
and then sailed away.

694
00:26:08,767 --> 00:26:10,800
- I don't know why
but I'm scared.

695
00:26:10,800 --> 00:26:12,033
Let's go back to the boat.

696
00:26:12,033 --> 00:26:13,367
- There's nothing
to be scared of.

697
00:26:13,367 --> 00:26:14,733
- Oh no, it'll only
take a few minutes.

698
00:26:14,733 --> 00:26:16,767
Then we'll be back at
the beach, come on.

699
00:26:16,767 --> 00:26:17,600
- Come on.

700
00:26:19,067 --> 00:26:21,100
- [Tom] It's few minutes and
we're not back at the beach!

701
00:26:22,933 --> 00:26:24,833
- [Mike] On his resume
Robert Reed listed this

702
00:26:24,833 --> 00:26:25,933
as "The Tempest."

703
00:26:28,167 --> 00:26:29,767
- [Crow] Oh, she's a Breck girl.

704
00:26:32,733 --> 00:26:35,367
- [Tom] Smells like James
Galway is bringing up the rear.

705
00:26:35,367 --> 00:26:36,267
- Whoa!

706
00:26:36,267 --> 00:26:37,500
- Johnny!

707
00:26:37,500 --> 00:26:38,500
- [Mike] How long do
we bake the clams?

708
00:26:41,367 --> 00:26:42,467
- Johnny!

709
00:26:42,467 --> 00:26:43,933
- [Crow] Ah, just bury him.

710
00:26:43,933 --> 00:26:45,167
- He looks hurt.

711
00:26:45,167 --> 00:26:46,100
- All right, I'll
get some vines.

712
00:26:46,100 --> 00:26:47,833
We can use them to pull him out.

713
00:26:47,833 --> 00:26:49,700
- [Tom] Or hang myself.

714
00:26:49,700 --> 00:26:51,133
- It was deliberately dug here.

715
00:26:51,133 --> 00:26:52,433
- But who could have done it?

716
00:26:52,433 --> 00:26:54,333
- Oh I don't know, but I do know

717
00:26:54,333 --> 00:26:55,867
that as soon as we get
him up outta that whole

718
00:26:55,867 --> 00:26:57,533
we're getting out of here.

719
00:26:57,533 --> 00:26:58,767
Wait a minute, Pete.

720
00:26:58,767 --> 00:26:59,967
I'll climb down.

721
00:26:59,967 --> 00:27:01,567
We'll need you here
to pull him up.

722
00:27:01,567 --> 00:27:03,833
- [Mike] Yeah, we'll need
your massive bulk up here.

723
00:27:07,300 --> 00:27:09,167
- [Crow] A little peak
into their home life.

724
00:27:09,167 --> 00:27:11,333
- [Tom] They should
just clam bake him.

725
00:27:11,333 --> 00:27:13,300
- [Mike] Yeah.

726
00:27:13,300 --> 00:27:14,900
- Okay, start pulling.

727
00:27:17,300 --> 00:27:19,133
- [Tom] Speaking of pulling.

728
00:27:19,133 --> 00:27:21,933
- [Crow] I'm here to tell
you about Mutual of Omaha.

729
00:27:21,933 --> 00:27:22,833
- [Tom] Oh.

730
00:27:26,033 --> 00:27:27,067
- Help them, quickly.

731
00:27:29,900 --> 00:27:33,000
(electronic beeping)

732
00:27:40,500 --> 00:27:43,100
- Wow, well seems
like a good idea

733
00:27:43,100 --> 00:27:46,300
and hey, good look with
your vegetable stand.

734
00:27:46,300 --> 00:27:50,000
I'm gonna go off and be
Kenny G so I'll see ya later.

735
00:27:50,000 --> 00:27:52,867
- Well, mighty
neighborly of you, Mike.

736
00:27:52,867 --> 00:27:53,700
Take her easy.

737
00:27:56,367 --> 00:27:58,567
Not much traffic here today, eh?

738
00:27:59,833 --> 00:28:01,533
I wonder if this was
the best location

739
00:28:01,533 --> 00:28:02,933
for my produce stand.

740
00:28:04,433 --> 00:28:06,433
Suppose not a lot of
people have cars here

741
00:28:06,433 --> 00:28:08,033
on the satellite of love.

742
00:28:08,933 --> 00:28:09,867
(car honking)

743
00:28:09,867 --> 00:28:12,267
Whoa, hey sounds like I got me

744
00:28:12,267 --> 00:28:14,867
a potential customer
coming down the road.

745
00:28:16,000 --> 00:28:18,467
Yeah, of course
the only guy I know

746
00:28:18,467 --> 00:28:20,700
who has a car
around these parts.

747
00:28:22,233 --> 00:28:23,367
Oh no!

748
00:28:23,367 --> 00:28:24,200
Oh no!

749
00:28:26,500 --> 00:28:28,767
Jeez, next time buy something!

750
00:28:31,200 --> 00:28:33,200
(light music)

751
00:28:33,200 --> 00:28:34,600
(honking)

752
00:28:34,600 --> 00:28:37,533
(light music)

753
00:28:37,533 --> 00:28:39,033
- Yes, I came here
shortly after the war.

754
00:28:39,033 --> 00:28:40,333
- [Tom] What did you do?

755
00:28:40,333 --> 00:28:41,300
- [Crow] You okay honey?

756
00:28:41,300 --> 00:28:42,867
- [Mike] Guys.

757
00:28:42,867 --> 00:28:45,867
- A world completely free from
any kind of outside pressure.

758
00:28:45,867 --> 00:28:47,467
- But what do you do here?

759
00:28:47,467 --> 00:28:50,200
- Yeah, how do you get any
kicks in a place like this?

760
00:28:50,200 --> 00:28:51,700
- Kicks?

761
00:28:51,700 --> 00:28:52,633
- [Tom] My kicks just keep
getting harder to find.

762
00:28:52,633 --> 00:28:54,633
- I have some diversions.

763
00:28:54,633 --> 00:28:55,900
- [Crow] Or perversions.

764
00:28:55,900 --> 00:28:57,500
- I've developed a kind
of passion for hunting.

765
00:28:57,500 --> 00:28:58,500
- [Mike] And fudge stripes.

766
00:28:58,500 --> 00:28:59,333
- So that's it.

767
00:28:59,333 --> 00:29:00,433
That's why the trap was.

768
00:29:00,433 --> 00:29:02,533
- No, no, you're quite wrong.

769
00:29:02,533 --> 00:29:03,833
You see this island
was once the stronghold

770
00:29:03,833 --> 00:29:06,167
of some renegades
from the mainland.

771
00:29:06,167 --> 00:29:09,100
There may be one or two of them
still lurking in the jungle.

772
00:29:09,100 --> 00:29:11,800
But that must have
been their handy work.

773
00:29:11,800 --> 00:29:14,000
- But all these different
animals, they can't be native

774
00:29:14,000 --> 00:29:14,833
to this island.

775
00:29:14,833 --> 00:29:15,667
- They weren't.

776
00:29:15,667 --> 00:29:16,900
- [Mike] Ya idiot.

777
00:29:16,900 --> 00:29:18,500
- But you must know what
money can accomplish.

778
00:29:18,500 --> 00:29:21,033
It's quite simple to import
any kind of wild beast

779
00:29:21,033 --> 00:29:22,500
from anywhere in the world.

780
00:29:22,500 --> 00:29:23,733
- [Tom] An odd production
of "Blithe Spirit."

781
00:29:23,733 --> 00:29:27,500
- Other men collect fame
and riches, I collect.

782
00:29:27,500 --> 00:29:28,600
- [Crow] Beer cans.

783
00:29:28,600 --> 00:29:29,700
- Trophies.

784
00:29:29,700 --> 00:29:31,300
- [Mike] Thank you.

785
00:29:31,300 --> 00:29:33,133
- Get out of my way.

786
00:29:33,133 --> 00:29:35,733
- [Crow] Oh Oscar, Oscar, Oscar.

787
00:29:37,133 --> 00:29:39,233
- I thought you had visitors, I.

788
00:29:39,233 --> 00:29:40,167
- [Crow] Kitty.

789
00:29:40,167 --> 00:29:41,933
- I hear these strange voices.

790
00:29:41,933 --> 00:29:43,600
- [Tom] There's a two
drunk minimum in this film.

791
00:29:43,600 --> 00:29:45,800
- Yes Dean, these young
people have stumbled

792
00:29:45,800 --> 00:29:48,167
unto my retreat.
- [Mike] All eight of 'em?

793
00:29:48,167 --> 00:29:52,833
- I see that you're
not feeling well again.

794
00:29:52,833 --> 00:29:53,667
- That's right.

795
00:29:54,600 --> 00:29:55,533
Maybe uh--

796
00:29:55,533 --> 00:29:56,700
- [Crow] Hey buzz off, pal.

797
00:29:56,700 --> 00:29:58,800
- Shouldn't bother
with introductions now.

798
00:30:00,033 --> 00:30:03,633
I'll go back to my room.

799
00:30:03,633 --> 00:30:05,467
- I think that would be best.

800
00:30:05,467 --> 00:30:07,967
You can meet our
young friends later.

801
00:30:07,967 --> 00:30:09,467
- [Tom] I threw up on
your water buffalo.

802
00:30:09,467 --> 00:30:10,667
I hope that's okay.

803
00:30:12,133 --> 00:30:13,567
- That's right.

804
00:30:13,567 --> 00:30:14,700
- [Mike] Because you
never get a second chance

805
00:30:14,700 --> 00:30:15,900
to make a first impression.

806
00:30:15,900 --> 00:30:19,133
- Meet our young friend later.

807
00:30:19,133 --> 00:30:19,967
- [Tom] Yes.

808
00:30:20,833 --> 00:30:22,233
- Later.

809
00:30:22,233 --> 00:30:24,400
- [Tom] Bye retired
grifter, I'm going to sleep.

810
00:30:25,933 --> 00:30:27,667
- [Mike] He's an odd duck.

811
00:30:27,667 --> 00:30:29,500
- Dean was once a
very brilliant man

812
00:30:29,500 --> 00:30:33,500
but I keep him here with me
because I feel sorry for him

813
00:30:33,500 --> 00:30:35,533
and he's an excellent
chess player.

814
00:30:35,533 --> 00:30:37,567
- [Crow] And he looks
terrific in a gingham skirt.

815
00:30:37,567 --> 00:30:38,867
- You'll all enjoy his
company when he's sober

816
00:30:38,867 --> 00:30:41,267
and can get a
chance to know him.

817
00:30:41,267 --> 00:30:42,500
- Well I'm sure
that's right sir,

818
00:30:42,500 --> 00:30:43,967
but I don't think
we'll get the chance.

819
00:30:43,967 --> 00:30:45,200
We have to leave now.

820
00:30:45,200 --> 00:30:46,767
You see, our boat's
anchored off the island.

821
00:30:46,767 --> 00:30:48,667
- I'm afraid that's quite
out of the question.

822
00:30:48,667 --> 00:30:49,900
You'd never make it through

823
00:30:49,900 --> 00:30:52,067
this animal infested
jungle tonight.

824
00:30:52,067 --> 00:30:54,133
- But our boat, you
see the man who owns it

825
00:30:54,133 --> 00:30:55,167
doesn't know we went ashore.

826
00:30:55,167 --> 00:30:56,533
And he's sure to wake up.

827
00:30:56,533 --> 00:30:58,367
- You can put your
minds at rest.

828
00:30:58,367 --> 00:31:00,200
I have a very competent staff.

829
00:31:00,200 --> 00:31:01,600
They'll take care of everything.

830
00:31:01,600 --> 00:31:02,533
- [Mike] We'll leave a light on.

831
00:31:02,533 --> 00:31:03,967
- Will you excuse me a moment?

832
00:31:03,967 --> 00:31:05,200
I'll be right back.

833
00:31:05,200 --> 00:31:06,667
- [Crow] I need to
check my script.

834
00:31:10,533 --> 00:31:11,767
(smacking lips)

835
00:31:11,767 --> 00:31:14,167
Good god look at
those people, mm.

836
00:31:14,167 --> 00:31:16,033
- [Tom] Hey everybody, guess
who's in my living room.

837
00:31:16,033 --> 00:31:17,200
Robert Reed.

838
00:31:17,200 --> 00:31:19,567
- Johnny, Johnny
what are we gonna do?

839
00:31:20,700 --> 00:31:22,233
This place gives me the creeps.

840
00:31:23,633 --> 00:31:26,100
- Oh I don't know, I'd kinda
like to see what gives.

841
00:31:26,100 --> 00:31:27,367
- Sure and what'd ya
thinks gonna happen

842
00:31:27,367 --> 00:31:28,933
when Tony wakes up
and finds us gone?

843
00:31:30,067 --> 00:31:31,500
I mean he'll head
straight back to port.

844
00:31:31,500 --> 00:31:34,033
Maybe remember to
report us missing.

845
00:31:34,033 --> 00:31:35,767
We're liable to be
stuck here for days.

846
00:31:35,767 --> 00:31:37,300
- Don't worry about that.

847
00:31:37,300 --> 00:31:39,767
Tony won't be going
anywhere without us.

848
00:31:39,767 --> 00:31:41,000
- What'd you mean?

849
00:31:41,000 --> 00:31:42,500
- [Mike] I don't
know what I mean.

850
00:31:42,500 --> 00:31:43,333
- I mean, I made sure of
that before we left the boat

851
00:31:43,333 --> 00:31:44,000
by taking the rotor.

852
00:31:45,433 --> 00:31:46,800
He won't be able to start
the engine without us.

853
00:31:46,800 --> 00:31:49,867
- Oh Pete, you're wonderful.

854
00:31:49,867 --> 00:31:51,067
- Well that takes
care of the boat but.

855
00:31:51,067 --> 00:31:52,800
- Hold it, he's coming back.

856
00:31:54,367 --> 00:31:56,700
- [Tom] I just changed
out of my smoking jacket.

857
00:31:57,900 --> 00:31:59,300
- [Crow] Don't even think it.

858
00:32:00,767 --> 00:32:03,067
- [Mike] She's got a whole
lot of face going on.

859
00:32:05,033 --> 00:32:06,200
- May I present my wife.

860
00:32:06,200 --> 00:32:07,400
- [Robots] Sure!

861
00:32:07,400 --> 00:32:08,533
- Sandra, Betty,

862
00:32:08,533 --> 00:32:09,767
- [Tom] Bear.

863
00:32:09,767 --> 00:32:12,533
- Jeanne, Johnny and Pete.

864
00:32:12,533 --> 00:32:13,700
I was sure that you would want

865
00:32:13,700 --> 00:32:14,800
to make them welcome
to our island.

866
00:32:14,800 --> 00:32:16,333
- [Mike] Welcome
to death island.

867
00:32:16,333 --> 00:32:19,067
- Naturally, I am
glad to see you.

868
00:32:19,067 --> 00:32:20,367
- [Crow] Did you bring Paxel?

869
00:32:20,367 --> 00:32:22,867
- Will you be
staying for awhile?

870
00:32:22,867 --> 00:32:23,800
- [Tom] Yeah!

871
00:32:23,800 --> 00:32:24,800
- Well you see Mrs. Balleau.

872
00:32:24,800 --> 00:32:26,300
- Yes they will, my dear.

873
00:32:26,300 --> 00:32:28,000
I think I've convinced
them of the foolishness

874
00:32:28,000 --> 00:32:30,033
of trying to leave tonight.

875
00:32:30,033 --> 00:32:31,867
- [Johnny] But we
don't want to impose.

876
00:32:31,867 --> 00:32:33,233
- It's our pleasure.

877
00:32:33,233 --> 00:32:34,733
- [Mike] We'll make a
meatloaf of some sort.

878
00:32:34,733 --> 00:32:36,433
- Isn't it Sandra?

879
00:32:36,433 --> 00:32:37,500
- Of course.

880
00:32:37,500 --> 00:32:38,867
- [Crow] He's working the jaw.

881
00:32:38,867 --> 00:32:40,600
- Now if you'll show the
girls the guest room.

882
00:32:40,600 --> 00:32:41,800
- [Tom] And the guest
to the girls room.

883
00:32:41,800 --> 00:32:44,267
- Yes, will you
come with me please?

884
00:32:44,267 --> 00:32:46,867
- [Crow] And see what the boys
in the backroom will have.

885
00:32:48,333 --> 00:32:50,567
- [Mike] It's Eva Braun's
bed and breakfast.

886
00:32:54,267 --> 00:32:55,300
- [Crow] Say, where'd
you get the babes?

887
00:32:55,300 --> 00:32:56,600
- I've made arrangements for you

888
00:32:56,600 --> 00:32:57,733
to spend a comfortable night.

889
00:32:57,733 --> 00:32:59,000
- [Tom] On me.

890
00:32:59,000 --> 00:33:00,233
- We can discuss your
leaving in the morning.

891
00:33:01,700 --> 00:33:03,167
- [Tom] Mr. Carlin.

892
00:33:03,167 --> 00:33:05,000
- [Crow] Hey Hartley,
sorry I'm late, ya idiots.

893
00:33:05,000 --> 00:33:06,733
- Prepared a room for you.

894
00:33:06,733 --> 00:33:07,900
He will take you to it.

895
00:33:09,067 --> 00:33:10,067
- Well I don't think we oughta--

896
00:33:10,067 --> 00:33:11,333
- Goodnight.

897
00:33:11,333 --> 00:33:12,800
- [Tom] Don't let
the bed bugs bite.

898
00:33:12,800 --> 00:33:15,300
- Don't worry, everything
will be taken care of.

899
00:33:15,300 --> 00:33:17,967
(crow cackling)

900
00:33:19,667 --> 00:33:21,900
- [Tom] Oh, and he has
a tendency to fondle

901
00:33:21,900 --> 00:33:22,967
so please be careful.

902
00:33:24,533 --> 00:33:26,133
- [Crow] Want turn down service?

903
00:33:28,133 --> 00:33:30,267
- [Mike] As for the
rest of you, oh.

904
00:33:32,233 --> 00:33:35,833
- [Crow] Now for some
microwave french fries.

905
00:33:35,833 --> 00:33:37,700
Well now how about me,
do I care for a drink.

906
00:33:37,700 --> 00:33:41,733
Well I oughtn't but, well
I'll have what I'm having.

907
00:33:43,000 --> 00:33:44,500
- [Mike] God, I love liquor.

908
00:33:44,500 --> 00:33:47,300
I should gain weight so I can
handle more of this stuff.

909
00:33:48,933 --> 00:33:50,467
- [Crow] White shoulders!

910
00:33:51,733 --> 00:33:53,200
Later that same drink.

911
00:33:54,367 --> 00:33:56,200
- [Tom] Wow, that
stuff really works.

912
00:33:58,200 --> 00:34:01,300
- [Crow] Nom, there's gin
on the corner of your mouth.

913
00:34:01,300 --> 00:34:02,833
Mm, oh I got it.

914
00:34:02,833 --> 00:34:05,233
- [Tom] Your lips are like
a crisp, delicious bacon.

915
00:34:08,467 --> 00:34:10,233
- Dean, we have to be careful.

916
00:34:10,233 --> 00:34:12,333
He could walk in any minute.

917
00:34:12,333 --> 00:34:14,133
And his spies are
all over the house.

918
00:34:14,133 --> 00:34:15,367
- I know.

919
00:34:15,367 --> 00:34:16,800
- [Crow] The lion
may be watching.

920
00:34:18,233 --> 00:34:20,933
- I can't take much more
of this madness, Sandra.

921
00:34:20,933 --> 00:34:22,633
I can't go on forever pretending

922
00:34:22,633 --> 00:34:25,000
to be a useless drunk.
- [Mike] It was fun for awhile

923
00:34:25,000 --> 00:34:26,400
but my liver keeps
shutting down.

924
00:34:26,400 --> 00:34:27,833
- Tonight we've got
to try to escape.

925
00:34:27,833 --> 00:34:30,433
We've got to try again.

926
00:34:30,433 --> 00:34:32,533
- But how Dean, how?

927
00:34:32,533 --> 00:34:34,033
- [Tom] Howdy how?

928
00:34:34,033 --> 00:34:36,800
- He can never allow anyone
to leave this place alive.

929
00:34:36,800 --> 00:34:39,267
And he has made sure
no one ever will.

930
00:34:39,267 --> 00:34:40,467
- I know.

931
00:34:40,467 --> 00:34:42,733
- [Crow] She's got more
face than she needs.

932
00:34:44,133 --> 00:34:47,933
- And now with these kids here,
I think we've got a chance.

933
00:34:47,933 --> 00:34:49,900
- What do you mean?

934
00:34:49,900 --> 00:34:52,467
- I've worked it
all out in my mind.

935
00:34:52,467 --> 00:34:55,367
- [Tom] Anyone seen
my A-deck, whoa.

936
00:34:55,367 --> 00:34:56,933
- [Mike] I was just talking
to Misses Nipple, I mean.

937
00:34:56,933 --> 00:35:01,100
- Now Sandra,
entertaining our guest?

938
00:35:01,100 --> 00:35:03,800
- [Tom] That's a trick
question, isn't it?

939
00:35:03,800 --> 00:35:06,333
He looks like an
Easter Island statue.

940
00:35:06,333 --> 00:35:08,967
- You've recovered from
your indisposition, I see.

941
00:35:08,967 --> 00:35:09,800
I'm glad.

942
00:35:10,833 --> 00:35:12,367
- Thank you.

943
00:35:12,367 --> 00:35:14,633
- I was just about to take
a turn around the grounds.

944
00:35:14,633 --> 00:35:16,800
Wouldn't you two
like to join me?

945
00:35:16,800 --> 00:35:18,900
We go out so seldom.

946
00:35:18,900 --> 00:35:22,700
- [Crow] We got this
thing we should probably.

947
00:35:22,700 --> 00:35:23,900
- I was about to go to my room.

948
00:35:23,900 --> 00:35:26,267
- [Tom] My face needs a rest.

949
00:35:26,267 --> 00:35:27,100
- Dean?

950
00:35:27,100 --> 00:35:29,133
- No, no thank you.

951
00:35:29,133 --> 00:35:31,367
- [Mike] I was about
to go to her room too.

952
00:35:31,367 --> 00:35:32,700
- I think I'll turn in too.

953
00:35:33,800 --> 00:35:35,400
- Then you won't
mind escorting Sandra

954
00:35:35,400 --> 00:35:37,267
to her room, would you?

955
00:35:37,267 --> 00:35:38,800
- [Tom] Oh ick, well all right.

956
00:35:41,000 --> 00:35:44,167
- [Crow] Allow me to accompany
you to my pants, I mean room.

957
00:35:45,067 --> 00:35:46,633
- Good night.

958
00:35:46,633 --> 00:35:48,467
- [Mike] Oh how banal.

959
00:35:48,467 --> 00:35:49,667
- Dean?

960
00:35:49,667 --> 00:35:50,867
- [Tom] I wasn't
looking at her butt!

961
00:35:50,867 --> 00:35:52,700
- I've worked out a
very satisfactory design

962
00:35:52,700 --> 00:35:53,667
for living here.

963
00:35:54,533 --> 00:35:55,900
I think you realize that.

964
00:35:57,100 --> 00:35:59,000
I hope you also realize
that the presence

965
00:35:59,000 --> 00:36:02,000
of those four young people
here changes nothing

966
00:36:02,000 --> 00:36:03,667
as far as we're concerned.

967
00:36:03,667 --> 00:36:05,700
- [Crow] And I'm
still Victor Buono.

968
00:36:05,700 --> 00:36:07,433
- I've taken care of everything.

969
00:36:10,300 --> 00:36:12,400
- [Mike] I don't think
he suspects a thing.

970
00:36:14,733 --> 00:36:16,600
(door closing)

971
00:36:16,600 --> 00:36:19,100
(light music)

972
00:36:22,667 --> 00:36:24,700
- You don't think we're
dreaming do you, Betty?

973
00:36:24,700 --> 00:36:26,100
- [Tom] It's Rob
and Laura's bedroom.

974
00:36:26,100 --> 00:36:27,967
- No, this is no dream.

975
00:36:27,967 --> 00:36:30,300
But I can't think of a
better explanation, either.

976
00:36:33,333 --> 00:36:36,000
- [Mike] It's the boys from
the camp across the lake.

977
00:36:37,167 --> 00:36:39,333
- [Tom] Oh this map, I
love to touch this map.

978
00:36:39,333 --> 00:36:40,167
- Come in.

979
00:36:44,367 --> 00:36:45,633
- Are you all right?

980
00:36:45,633 --> 00:36:47,267
- So far we are but I
just can't understand

981
00:36:47,267 --> 00:36:48,667
what's happening to us.

982
00:36:48,667 --> 00:36:50,167
- Neither can I.

983
00:36:50,167 --> 00:36:51,433
- That big guy that took
us to our room up the hall

984
00:36:51,433 --> 00:36:52,800
wasn't very friendly.

985
00:36:52,800 --> 00:36:54,333
- I've got a feeling
this isn't as friendly

986
00:36:54,333 --> 00:36:56,433
a place as that Mr.
Balleau tries to make out.

987
00:36:56,433 --> 00:36:57,667
- [Tom] Shut up.

988
00:36:57,667 --> 00:36:58,667
- I still can't figure
out how he got here

989
00:36:58,667 --> 00:37:00,633
on the island in
the first place.

990
00:37:00,633 --> 00:37:02,200
- Well I figure this
house was first built

991
00:37:02,200 --> 00:37:04,000
by one of those, you know,
crackpot millionaires

992
00:37:04,000 --> 00:37:05,433
about 100 years ago.

993
00:37:05,433 --> 00:37:08,333
When Balleau found out
about it he sorta moved in.

994
00:37:08,333 --> 00:37:09,400
- You know that could be it.

995
00:37:09,400 --> 00:37:11,000
He said he had a lot of money.

996
00:37:11,000 --> 00:37:14,800
- But why, oh I still don't
know what's going on here.

997
00:37:14,800 --> 00:37:16,000
- [Crow] Neither do we!

998
00:37:16,000 --> 00:37:17,400
- Well it's a cinch
nobody's gonna tell us.

999
00:37:17,400 --> 00:37:18,733
So we'll just have to
find out for ourselves.

1000
00:37:18,733 --> 00:37:20,200
- [Pete] Yeah, but how?

1001
00:37:20,200 --> 00:37:21,700
- Let's split up and
have a look around.

1002
00:37:21,700 --> 00:37:22,533
- [Tom] Yeah, but how?

1003
00:37:22,533 --> 00:37:24,067
- Oh I don't know.

1004
00:37:24,067 --> 00:37:25,800
I'm kinda scared.

1005
00:37:25,800 --> 00:37:27,333
- Well there's nothing
to be scared about.

1006
00:37:27,333 --> 00:37:28,633
I mean, I'll be with ya.

1007
00:37:28,633 --> 00:37:30,000
- [Mike] My point exactly.

1008
00:37:30,000 --> 00:37:32,333
- And Betty, you'll be with me.

1009
00:37:32,333 --> 00:37:35,700
Now listen, don't get into
anything you can't handle.

1010
00:37:35,700 --> 00:37:38,067
If you find out anything
head straight back here

1011
00:37:38,067 --> 00:37:39,933
and we'll do the same, okay?

1012
00:37:39,933 --> 00:37:41,233
- Yup.

1013
00:37:41,233 --> 00:37:43,000
- [Crow] See ya at the
office on Monday, then.

1014
00:37:44,533 --> 00:37:47,000
- [Tom] Okay, kids let's
go sell that band candy.

1015
00:37:48,067 --> 00:37:50,433
(humming)

1016
00:37:50,433 --> 00:37:52,367
- [Mike] Is it time
to report back yet?

1017
00:37:54,467 --> 00:37:56,000
- Okay, this is where we split.

1018
00:37:56,000 --> 00:37:57,900
Pete, you and
Jeanne go that way.

1019
00:37:57,900 --> 00:37:59,400
- Okay.

1020
00:37:59,400 --> 00:38:00,767
- [Crow] Robert Reed's part
makes it look like he has

1021
00:38:00,767 --> 00:38:01,900
a lid on his head.

1022
00:38:03,700 --> 00:38:06,167
- [Tom] I think she's shorter
than she was a minute ago.

1023
00:38:09,533 --> 00:38:12,400
- [Mike] Do you have a few
minutes to take a survey?

1024
00:38:12,400 --> 00:38:13,333
- I promise to
release you but only

1025
00:38:13,333 --> 00:38:14,567
if you swear not to cry out.

1026
00:38:14,567 --> 00:38:16,433
- [Crow] And quit
licking my hand.

1027
00:38:16,433 --> 00:38:19,500
- All right, go
back to your room.

1028
00:38:19,500 --> 00:38:20,433
We'll go with you.

1029
00:38:20,433 --> 00:38:21,967
We've got to talk to you.

1030
00:38:22,900 --> 00:38:24,067
- What's this all about?

1031
00:38:24,067 --> 00:38:25,233
- Please go back to your room.

1032
00:38:25,233 --> 00:38:26,800
We cannot talk here.

1033
00:38:26,800 --> 00:38:28,933
Please, there is
death all around us.

1034
00:38:28,933 --> 00:38:30,533
- [Tom] Well, you
could crack a window.

1035
00:38:30,533 --> 00:38:32,567
- Okay, Betty come on.

1036
00:38:32,567 --> 00:38:33,400
- Hurry.

1037
00:38:34,567 --> 00:38:36,167
- [Crow] They progressed
a full 10 feet

1038
00:38:36,167 --> 00:38:37,367
before being captured.

1039
00:38:40,100 --> 00:38:43,333
- [Mike] Careful, there's death
all around us in here too.

1040
00:38:43,333 --> 00:38:45,433
- [Tom] No, we don't have
money for other sets.

1041
00:38:47,267 --> 00:38:48,100
- Well?

1042
00:38:49,233 --> 00:38:50,267
- There's something
I wanna tell ya.

1043
00:38:50,267 --> 00:38:51,500
- [Tom] Radishes make me burp.

1044
00:38:51,500 --> 00:38:52,933
- Unless you do
exactly as I say,

1045
00:38:52,933 --> 00:38:54,133
you'll never get
off this island.

1046
00:38:54,133 --> 00:38:55,800
- [Mike] There,
I've said my piece.

1047
00:38:57,167 --> 00:38:59,167
- [Tom] Hm, it looks
like Liberace's in there.

1048
00:39:01,033 --> 00:39:03,867
- [Crow] Indiana nerd
and the temple of dork!

1049
00:39:03,867 --> 00:39:06,367
(light music)

1050
00:39:07,767 --> 00:39:09,500
- [Mike] Am I waiting okay
or should I, and wait,

1051
00:39:09,500 --> 00:39:10,767
how's this, I'm waiting.

1052
00:39:11,767 --> 00:39:14,433
(ominous music)

1053
00:39:16,700 --> 00:39:17,633
- [Robots] Surprise!

1054
00:39:17,633 --> 00:39:20,600
♪ Happy birthday to you♪

1055
00:39:20,600 --> 00:39:22,900
- [Crow] There's a whole
nother room in there.

1056
00:39:24,633 --> 00:39:26,000
- There's a stairway
there that leads down

1057
00:39:26,000 --> 00:39:27,533
into some kind of a tunnel.

1058
00:39:27,533 --> 00:39:28,400
- So?

1059
00:39:28,400 --> 00:39:29,633
- [Crow] Sue?

1060
00:39:29,633 --> 00:39:32,833
- Come on, let's
see where it goes.

1061
00:39:32,833 --> 00:39:34,533
- Please listen to him.

1062
00:39:34,533 --> 00:39:36,933
- [Crow] Awight, I'm wistening.

1063
00:39:36,933 --> 00:39:38,300
- And ourselves.

1064
00:39:38,300 --> 00:39:39,767
- I don't understand
any of this.

1065
00:39:39,767 --> 00:39:41,633
- Well I'll try and explain
it as quickly as I can.

1066
00:39:41,633 --> 00:39:42,867
- [Tom] What was there's this,

1067
00:39:42,867 --> 00:39:44,700
the wind began to
twitch and then.

1068
00:39:44,700 --> 00:39:45,767
- But they're not there.

1069
00:39:45,767 --> 00:39:47,000
- What?

1070
00:39:47,000 --> 00:39:48,233
- Well they're out in
the house somewhere.

1071
00:39:48,233 --> 00:39:49,433
They're trying to find
out what's going on.

1072
00:39:49,433 --> 00:39:50,133
Just as we were
when you stopped us.

1073
00:39:51,200 --> 00:39:52,633
- That's bad.

1074
00:39:52,633 --> 00:39:53,467
- Oh I'll go look for 'em.

1075
00:39:53,467 --> 00:39:54,967
- No, no, stay here.

1076
00:39:54,967 --> 00:39:58,100
- [Tom] One of the stupidest
hostages we've ever had here.

1077
00:39:58,100 --> 00:40:00,900
- Let's just pray that
they'll be all right.

1078
00:40:00,900 --> 00:40:03,433
- [Crow] This is where they
kneel down with Kissinger.

1079
00:40:05,000 --> 00:40:06,833
(light music)

1080
00:40:06,833 --> 00:40:08,533
- [Mike] What'd ya
know, a microbrewery.

1081
00:40:08,533 --> 00:40:10,333
Of course, I can't drink.

1082
00:40:10,333 --> 00:40:12,867
- [Crow] An entire cave
made out of garbage bags.

1083
00:40:15,600 --> 00:40:17,000
- [Mike] Are we
over investigating?

1084
00:40:17,000 --> 00:40:18,233
We need to ask ourselves that.

1085
00:40:18,233 --> 00:40:20,567
We should probably hide
under the bed instead.

1086
00:40:20,567 --> 00:40:22,567
- [Tom] You're
constantly clammy, Steve.

1087
00:40:26,433 --> 00:40:28,967
(screaming)

1088
00:40:28,967 --> 00:40:30,400
- [Mike] Cadavers for Algernon.

1089
00:40:30,400 --> 00:40:32,000
- [Crow] She's
finally the size five

1090
00:40:32,000 --> 00:40:33,300
she's always wanted to be.

1091
00:40:34,800 --> 00:40:37,567
- [Tom] Ow, ow, Susan you're
squeezing my booster shot.

1092
00:40:40,967 --> 00:40:43,533
- [Mike] Come on, it's just
a Jaycees Haunted House.

1093
00:40:45,733 --> 00:40:48,500
- [Crow] The wall is covered
with Issey Miyake clothing.

1094
00:40:49,467 --> 00:40:51,067
- [Mike] Steve Allen, PI.

1095
00:40:53,033 --> 00:40:55,367
(ominous music)

1096
00:40:55,367 --> 00:40:57,533
- [Tom] I know, we'll
throw Gatorade at 'em.

1097
00:41:02,167 --> 00:41:03,667
- [Mike] Okay Mr. Villashez.

1098
00:41:03,667 --> 00:41:05,867
Come along, we'll get you
back to your room here.

1099
00:41:08,067 --> 00:41:09,000
- [Crow] I wonder what he scores

1100
00:41:09,000 --> 00:41:10,500
on the Myers-Briggs inventory.

1101
00:41:11,633 --> 00:41:13,933
(ominous music)

1102
00:41:13,933 --> 00:41:16,533
- [Mike] Okay, I've decided
that phase B of our plan

1103
00:41:16,533 --> 00:41:17,800
involves a lot of hiding.

1104
00:41:22,500 --> 00:41:23,333
- [Crow] Hi.

1105
00:41:24,567 --> 00:41:26,867
- [Tom] Love this job,
I can dress how I want.

1106
00:41:29,300 --> 00:41:31,300
- [Mike] Gulp.

1107
00:41:31,300 --> 00:41:33,900
- [Crow] Huh, when did I
have teenagers installed?

1108
00:41:37,100 --> 00:41:38,900
- [Mike] Oh man, if
he had caught 'em

1109
00:41:38,900 --> 00:41:40,467
it woulda been really awkward

1110
00:41:40,467 --> 00:41:42,367
because they haven't
been introduced yet.

1111
00:41:44,100 --> 00:41:45,500
- [Crow] This would
be really suspenseful

1112
00:41:45,500 --> 00:41:47,033
but for the fact that it isn't.

1113
00:41:48,533 --> 00:41:51,033
Oh no, they've stumbled
unto another room!

1114
00:41:52,967 --> 00:41:54,233
- [Mike] Well, there's
that air hockey game

1115
00:41:54,233 --> 00:41:55,833
that we gave him
that he never uses

1116
00:41:55,833 --> 00:41:57,033
and the rowing machine.

1117
00:41:59,967 --> 00:42:01,933
- [Tom] Oh, it's their
cotton candy lab.

1118
00:42:04,600 --> 00:42:06,600
Well are we having
the clam bake or not?

1119
00:42:08,333 --> 00:42:09,800
- [Crow] Quick, into the vat.

1120
00:42:14,467 --> 00:42:17,400
- [Tom] Ah, he's
changing the kitty liter.

1121
00:42:17,400 --> 00:42:18,900
- [Crow] Mm, nice back porch.

1122
00:42:18,900 --> 00:42:23,533
Oh crap, apple.

1123
00:42:23,533 --> 00:42:24,367
Hi again.

1124
00:42:25,933 --> 00:42:28,633
- [Tom] Employees must wash
hands before leaving dungeon.

1125
00:42:31,567 --> 00:42:33,867
- [Crow] Claussen Kosher
ladies, never cooked.

1126
00:42:35,500 --> 00:42:36,967
Always crisp.

1127
00:42:36,967 --> 00:42:38,567
- [Tom] Uh, I gotta limit
myself to about three seconds

1128
00:42:38,567 --> 00:42:39,500
of looking at that.

1129
00:42:41,633 --> 00:42:44,333
- [Mike] Well, time to
rinse out my silk things.

1130
00:42:47,100 --> 00:42:50,467
- [Crow] Man, if it weren't
for my hobbies I'd go nuts.

1131
00:42:50,467 --> 00:42:52,833
- [Tom] He's gonna make
some of his famous toe jam.

1132
00:42:52,833 --> 00:42:53,800
(groaning)

1133
00:42:53,800 --> 00:42:55,300
- [Crow] That's just so wrong.

1134
00:42:55,300 --> 00:42:56,100
- [Tom] Sorry.

1135
00:42:59,367 --> 00:43:00,633
- [Mike] Oh it shrunk.

1136
00:43:04,267 --> 00:43:07,400
- [Crow] Went a little too
far with the derm abrasion.

1137
00:43:07,400 --> 00:43:10,100
- [Tom] I usually don't look
good in flesh tones, but.

1138
00:43:11,900 --> 00:43:14,033
- [Crow] He-he-he, that's funny.

1139
00:43:14,967 --> 00:43:16,200
- [Tom] Jackie Mason?

1140
00:43:16,200 --> 00:43:17,700
- [Crow] What are you
doing with my head?

1141
00:43:17,700 --> 00:43:19,300
This head should not be here
it should be on Broadway.

1142
00:43:19,300 --> 00:43:22,333
- [Mike] This is one of
Jeff Smith's weirder shows.

1143
00:43:22,333 --> 00:43:24,667
- [Crow] It's fun to
watch a craftsman at work.

1144
00:43:26,933 --> 00:43:28,400
- [Tom] There we go,
just a little more,

1145
00:43:28,400 --> 00:43:30,967
there one of my best ones
if I do say so myself.

1146
00:43:32,033 --> 00:43:33,500
(slurping)

1147
00:43:33,500 --> 00:43:35,867
- [Mike] Now be sure to save
the giblets for the cat.

1148
00:43:35,867 --> 00:43:37,867
Now Basques are one
of the finest people

1149
00:43:37,867 --> 00:43:40,133
and they do wonderful
things with organ meat.

1150
00:43:41,333 --> 00:43:42,567
- [Crow] Oh, this
is too fattening.

1151
00:43:42,567 --> 00:43:44,400
I'll get rid of it so
I won't be tempted.

1152
00:43:45,567 --> 00:43:47,433
- [Tom] Now to go
crunch some numbers.

1153
00:43:49,533 --> 00:43:52,433
- [Crow] Long before Peter
Billingsley there was this guy.

1154
00:43:54,267 --> 00:43:56,300
We are not staying for dinner.

1155
00:43:58,467 --> 00:43:59,433
- [Tom] Would you, I.

1156
00:43:59,433 --> 00:44:00,433
- [Crow] Try and get outta my--

1157
00:44:00,433 --> 00:44:02,067
- [Tom] I can't, I hear you.

1158
00:44:02,067 --> 00:44:03,633
- I can't believe it.

1159
00:44:03,633 --> 00:44:05,300
It's just too awful.

1160
00:44:05,300 --> 00:44:06,533
- [Tom] Poor Lonnie.

1161
00:44:06,533 --> 00:44:08,200
- Every word is
true, I swear it.

1162
00:44:08,200 --> 00:44:09,567
- You have to believe us.

1163
00:44:09,567 --> 00:44:11,700
- [Crow] Ty-D-Bol really works.

1164
00:44:11,700 --> 00:44:12,900
- Not a word about this.

1165
00:44:14,533 --> 00:44:16,000
- Somebody, give me a hand.

1166
00:44:16,000 --> 00:44:18,367
- [Mike] Bad news, the
kitchen's not up to code.

1167
00:44:18,367 --> 00:44:19,933
- Jeanne, oh!

1168
00:44:21,100 --> 00:44:22,533
- You'll never
believe what we saw.

1169
00:44:22,533 --> 00:44:23,567
- [Mike] Boiled heads?

1170
00:44:23,567 --> 00:44:25,067
- I don't believe it myself.

1171
00:44:25,067 --> 00:44:26,767
- We just heard about it.

1172
00:44:26,767 --> 00:44:27,933
I guess it's true then.

1173
00:44:29,367 --> 00:44:30,733
- If they told you about
an unbelievable nightmare

1174
00:44:30,733 --> 00:44:32,633
it's true all right.

1175
00:44:32,633 --> 00:44:33,867
- Then that settles it.

1176
00:44:33,867 --> 00:44:35,800
We've got to try to get
out of here tonight.

1177
00:44:35,800 --> 00:44:37,067
- Tonight?

1178
00:44:37,067 --> 00:44:38,267
- [Crow] Let's just see
how the skin turns out.

1179
00:44:38,267 --> 00:44:39,467
- Sandra and I have
been saying that

1180
00:44:39,467 --> 00:44:42,567
to each other every
night for the last year.

1181
00:44:42,567 --> 00:44:44,100
- Why haven't you ever tried?

1182
00:44:44,100 --> 00:44:45,767
- Never had the opportunity.

1183
00:44:45,767 --> 00:44:48,900
Balleau has always kept
us pretty well covered.

1184
00:44:48,900 --> 00:44:50,400
- What can we do?

1185
00:44:50,400 --> 00:44:52,600
- Well I have a plan that
I think will give us all

1186
00:44:52,600 --> 00:44:53,800
a good chance.

1187
00:44:53,800 --> 00:44:55,667
- [Tom] We all dress
like Shirley Valentine.

1188
00:44:55,667 --> 00:44:57,633
- You and Betty stay here
and watch after Jeanne

1189
00:44:57,633 --> 00:44:59,533
and be sure she's all right.

1190
00:44:59,533 --> 00:45:00,867
Johnny I want you
and Peter to meet me

1191
00:45:00,867 --> 00:45:02,800
in the trophy room
in a little while.

1192
00:45:02,800 --> 00:45:04,333
Oh it's all right.

1193
00:45:04,333 --> 00:45:05,267
We have the run of the house,
only be careful what you say.

1194
00:45:05,267 --> 00:45:06,700
- Yeah but what about Balleau?

1195
00:45:06,700 --> 00:45:08,367
- You don't have to worry about
him for a couple of hours.

1196
00:45:08,367 --> 00:45:09,900
- [Mike] Yeah, he's in the can.

1197
00:45:09,900 --> 00:45:11,467
- He's out in the jungle again.

1198
00:45:12,300 --> 00:45:14,333
After another trophy.

1199
00:45:14,333 --> 00:45:16,833
(light music)

1200
00:45:19,900 --> 00:45:21,800
- Yee haw, Cambot!

1201
00:45:21,800 --> 00:45:24,300
(funky music)

1202
00:45:27,267 --> 00:45:29,633
Now get in a line, get in a row,

1203
00:45:29,633 --> 00:45:32,067
grab your partner, here we go!

1204
00:45:32,067 --> 00:45:33,933
Swing her high
and swing her low.

1205
00:45:33,933 --> 00:45:35,833
Don't step on that
pretty little toe.

1206
00:45:35,833 --> 00:45:37,633
Now promenade.

1207
00:45:39,567 --> 00:45:41,633
Woo hoo, yee haw.

1208
00:45:41,633 --> 00:45:43,300
Eating turkey,
eating corn bread.

1209
00:45:43,300 --> 00:45:45,767
Jeans, skinny jeans
real well fed.

1210
00:45:45,767 --> 00:45:47,500
Allemande left and
allemande right.

1211
00:45:47,500 --> 00:45:51,400
Allemande left and allemande
right, now promenade.

1212
00:45:51,400 --> 00:45:52,467
Woo hoo.

1213
00:45:52,467 --> 00:45:53,400
- Yee haw!

1214
00:45:53,400 --> 00:45:54,233
- Woo hoo!

1215
00:45:57,167 --> 00:45:58,767
- Allemande right
and form a chain now,

1216
00:45:58,767 --> 00:46:01,300
form a star and
circulate to your left.

1217
00:46:01,300 --> 00:46:04,833
Ladies train now,
dosey doe allemande

1218
00:46:04,833 --> 00:46:08,933
and kick ball change
'em, now promenade.

1219
00:46:08,933 --> 00:46:10,333
Woo hoo, woo hoo!

1220
00:46:11,900 --> 00:46:13,767
Shake for breakfast,
shake for lunch,

1221
00:46:13,767 --> 00:46:16,233
a sensible meal
that you can munch!

1222
00:46:16,233 --> 00:46:19,567
Lowest prices all the time,
refreshing splash of lemon lime.

1223
00:46:19,567 --> 00:46:22,233
They closed the market,
the factories too.

1224
00:46:22,233 --> 00:46:24,967
Not just England it's
a cruel world, pretty.

1225
00:46:24,967 --> 00:46:28,700
Pretty like it, there's
no future, no future, ah!

1226
00:46:28,700 --> 00:46:31,200
(screaming)

1227
00:46:31,200 --> 00:46:32,633
Anarchy, anarchy!

1228
00:46:34,033 --> 00:46:35,300
Now promenade.

1229
00:46:36,633 --> 00:46:38,067
(klaxon alarm)

1230
00:46:38,067 --> 00:46:41,000
- Oh we got movie time, woo!

1231
00:46:41,000 --> 00:46:43,500
(funky music)

1232
00:46:47,300 --> 00:46:50,367
(electronic beeping)

1233
00:46:52,833 --> 00:46:54,767
- [Tom] Yee hoo!

1234
00:46:54,767 --> 00:46:57,000
- [Crow] Stop it, I'm
done with that game.

1235
00:46:57,000 --> 00:46:58,267
- Does he go out every night?

1236
00:46:58,267 --> 00:46:59,633
- Yes, every night
that he can hunt.

1237
00:46:59,633 --> 00:47:00,600
- [Tom] Wow, how
can he afford it?

1238
00:47:00,600 --> 00:47:01,967
- What, I mean who?

1239
00:47:01,967 --> 00:47:04,200
- Who, out there in that jungle

1240
00:47:04,200 --> 00:47:05,033
there are already several men

1241
00:47:05,033 --> 00:47:06,067
that he's driven insane.

1242
00:47:06,067 --> 00:47:07,300
- [Tom] With his low prices.

1243
00:47:07,300 --> 00:47:09,333
- And now there's
also, probably,

1244
00:47:09,333 --> 00:47:10,700
the owner of your boat.

1245
00:47:10,700 --> 00:47:11,967
- Tony?

1246
00:47:11,967 --> 00:47:12,833
- Well he must still
be out on the boat,

1247
00:47:12,833 --> 00:47:14,033
the engines won't work.

1248
00:47:14,033 --> 00:47:16,000
- No, well I'm sure that
by now Balleau's men

1249
00:47:16,000 --> 00:47:19,267
have taken that boat to the
other side of the island

1250
00:47:19,267 --> 00:47:20,567
to the hidden cove.

1251
00:47:20,567 --> 00:47:22,367
- Hidden cove,
well where's that?

1252
00:47:22,367 --> 00:47:23,667
- [Tom] I don't
know it's hidden!

1253
00:47:23,667 --> 00:47:26,400
- That's where your only
chance to escape lies.

1254
00:47:26,400 --> 00:47:27,433
- How do you mean?

1255
00:47:27,433 --> 00:47:28,633
- [Mike] Pause.

1256
00:47:28,633 --> 00:47:31,033
- There are several
boats moored off shore.

1257
00:47:31,033 --> 00:47:32,500
- [Mike] Pause.

1258
00:47:32,500 --> 00:47:34,633
- Someone will have to swim
out to one of them and.

1259
00:47:34,633 --> 00:47:35,867
- [Mike] Pause.

1260
00:47:35,867 --> 00:47:37,100
- Try to make it to the
mainland to get help.

1261
00:47:37,100 --> 00:47:39,433
- Well I'm not much of
a swimmer but I'll try.

1262
00:47:39,433 --> 00:47:40,700
- No, no.

1263
00:47:40,700 --> 00:47:41,500
- [Tom] You ain't
much of anything.

1264
00:47:41,500 --> 00:47:42,433
- I'll do that myself.

1265
00:47:42,433 --> 00:47:43,733
And Sandra will come with me.

1266
00:47:43,733 --> 00:47:44,967
- Sandra?

1267
00:47:44,967 --> 00:47:46,500
- That's right.

1268
00:47:46,500 --> 00:47:48,033
She's an excellent swimmer.
- [Tom] And a damn good cop.

1269
00:47:48,033 --> 00:47:49,800
- And even if we don't make it.

1270
00:47:49,800 --> 00:47:51,767
- [Crow] The Secretary
will disavow any knowledge

1271
00:47:51,767 --> 00:47:52,967
of our actions.

1272
00:47:52,967 --> 00:47:54,167
- Drowning would be
better than what Balleau

1273
00:47:54,167 --> 00:47:56,100
has in mind for both of us.

1274
00:47:56,100 --> 00:47:57,800
- [Mike] It'd just
be really bad.

1275
00:47:57,800 --> 00:47:59,633
It's alls that I'm saying.

1276
00:47:59,633 --> 00:48:01,667
- Now where do we
fit into all of this?

1277
00:48:01,667 --> 00:48:04,367
- [Tom] Where do we
fit into all of this?

1278
00:48:04,367 --> 00:48:06,367
- [Crow] Man his shirt is so
tight you can see his liver.

1279
00:48:06,367 --> 00:48:07,833
- You're gonna have
to cover for us.

1280
00:48:07,833 --> 00:48:10,400
If the guards find us gone
divert their attention

1281
00:48:10,400 --> 00:48:12,467
until we get to the boat.

1282
00:48:12,467 --> 00:48:15,000
And after that, well
after that I'm afraid

1283
00:48:15,000 --> 00:48:16,233
you'll have to look
out for yourselves

1284
00:48:16,233 --> 00:48:18,067
until I can come back with help.

1285
00:48:18,067 --> 00:48:20,100
- [Tom] Oh, fine, can
we end the scene please?

1286
00:48:21,300 --> 00:48:22,467
- Johnny?

1287
00:48:22,467 --> 00:48:23,733
- [Crow] What?

1288
00:48:23,733 --> 00:48:25,000
- I know it doesn't
sound very promising

1289
00:48:25,000 --> 00:48:26,767
but believe me it's the
only chance you've got.

1290
00:48:26,767 --> 00:48:27,600
- [Tom] What is?

1291
00:48:28,967 --> 00:48:30,667
- Well, I guess we'll
have to take it.

1292
00:48:30,667 --> 00:48:31,833
(exhaling)

1293
00:48:31,833 --> 00:48:33,833
- Good, now listen to me.

1294
00:48:33,833 --> 00:48:35,033
The first thing we do.

1295
00:48:35,033 --> 00:48:35,967
- [Mike] Let's kill
all the lawyers.

1296
00:48:35,967 --> 00:48:38,900
- Is go over to the north shore.

1297
00:48:38,900 --> 00:48:40,867
- [Crow] Arr, I dreamed
I went to Mandalay.

1298
00:48:40,867 --> 00:48:42,267
Arr, Jim boy.

1299
00:48:45,700 --> 00:48:48,033
- [Mike] Dating at the
College of St. Catherine.

1300
00:48:51,267 --> 00:48:53,000
- [Tom] That looks
like President Bush.

1301
00:48:53,000 --> 00:48:55,567
- [Crow] I tell ya,
honey, Barbara's got
eyes like an eagle.

1302
00:48:59,100 --> 00:49:02,233
- [Mike] Well I just had a wry
conversation, I'll tell ya.

1303
00:49:02,233 --> 00:49:03,733
- Did they get away?

1304
00:49:03,733 --> 00:49:05,600
- So far so good. I went as
far as the front door with 'em

1305
00:49:05,600 --> 00:49:06,800
and nobody saw me.

1306
00:49:06,800 --> 00:49:08,367
- Well those guards must
be getting careless.

1307
00:49:08,367 --> 00:49:09,700
One of 'em walked
right by Sandra's room

1308
00:49:09,700 --> 00:49:11,000
without checking it.

1309
00:49:11,000 --> 00:49:11,833
- Hold it.

1310
00:49:13,000 --> 00:49:14,567
- [Tom] Hi, we're
outta the sea bass.

1311
00:49:14,567 --> 00:49:15,833
Just thought I'd tell ya.

1312
00:49:17,400 --> 00:49:20,400
- [Mike] My name's Whistlin'
Pete if you need anything.

1313
00:49:20,400 --> 00:49:21,633
- Do you think he knows?

1314
00:49:21,633 --> 00:49:23,367
- I don't know but
Dean and Sandra

1315
00:49:23,367 --> 00:49:24,833
have had time to
get into the jungle.

1316
00:49:24,833 --> 00:49:26,067
- [Crow] You think
they'll bungle?

1317
00:49:26,067 --> 00:49:27,767
- Let's hope they
make it to the cove.

1318
00:49:28,967 --> 00:49:31,600
- [Tom] What, oh damn,
no lifeguard on duty.

1319
00:49:31,600 --> 00:49:32,800
- Do you think we're too softy.

1320
00:49:32,800 --> 00:49:34,200
- Now wait a minute,

1321
00:49:34,200 --> 00:49:36,467
I just wanna make
sure of the direction.

1322
00:49:36,467 --> 00:49:38,667
That guard won't
be back right away.

1323
00:49:38,667 --> 00:49:39,967
Yes, that's it.

1324
00:49:39,967 --> 00:49:40,800
- Dean.

1325
00:49:40,800 --> 00:49:41,967
- What is it?

1326
00:49:41,967 --> 00:49:43,500
I may never get a chance
to say this again.

1327
00:49:43,500 --> 00:49:44,567
- [Crow] Never wear a speedo.

1328
00:49:44,567 --> 00:49:46,500
- I love you with all my heart.

1329
00:49:46,500 --> 00:49:49,067
- [Crow] I hope
we'll boil together.

1330
00:49:49,067 --> 00:49:50,233
I wasn't finished.

1331
00:49:50,233 --> 00:49:53,200
- Darling, we won't fail.

1332
00:49:54,200 --> 00:49:56,033
We can't fail.

1333
00:49:56,033 --> 00:49:57,700
- [Mike] Ew, he's
unhinging his jaw.

1334
00:49:57,700 --> 00:49:59,267
- [Tom] I love to lamprey.

1335
00:50:00,567 --> 00:50:01,400
- Come on.

1336
00:50:02,733 --> 00:50:04,567
(dramatic music)

1337
00:50:04,567 --> 00:50:07,933
- [Crow] Well, time for
a little pole vaulting.

1338
00:50:07,933 --> 00:50:09,267
- [Mike] Well shouldn't
have stopped back there

1339
00:50:09,267 --> 00:50:10,967
this is a much
more dramatic shot.

1340
00:50:12,167 --> 00:50:14,133
- [Crow] Hey, he's
got an ugly stick.

1341
00:50:14,133 --> 00:50:17,133
- [Tom] The new Banana Republic
mature fit expands with you.

1342
00:50:21,233 --> 00:50:23,567
- [Mike] When are we
gonna have that clam bake!

1343
00:50:24,700 --> 00:50:26,700
- [Crow] Oo, that's cold.

1344
00:50:26,700 --> 00:50:28,200
- [Mike] Looks like my
grandma's house toward

1345
00:50:28,200 --> 00:50:29,200
the end of her life.

1346
00:50:31,400 --> 00:50:33,700
- [Tom] I splashed
some water on my pits.

1347
00:50:33,700 --> 00:50:34,967
- Did you find anything?

1348
00:50:34,967 --> 00:50:36,033
- No, I listened to
two guards talking.

1349
00:50:36,033 --> 00:50:37,533
One of them went
over to the cove

1350
00:50:37,533 --> 00:50:39,300
but he didn't mention
anything about the boats.

1351
00:50:39,300 --> 00:50:40,133
- It's been two days.

1352
00:50:40,133 --> 00:50:40,967
- [Crow] She grew!

1353
00:50:40,967 --> 00:50:41,933
- And no word.

1354
00:50:41,933 --> 00:50:43,233
- What I can't understand

1355
00:50:43,233 --> 00:50:45,533
is why we haven't heard
from Balleau before now.

1356
00:50:45,533 --> 00:50:46,367
- You don't think?

1357
00:50:46,367 --> 00:50:47,333
- [Mike] Yes, I do.

1358
00:50:47,333 --> 00:50:48,633
- I don't know what to think.

1359
00:50:49,533 --> 00:50:51,733
Hold it, somebody's coming.

1360
00:50:55,500 --> 00:50:57,267
- [Tom] Here's your
towels, Hartley.

1361
00:50:58,833 --> 00:51:01,267
- [Crow] Ah let's see,
which room had the rumaki?

1362
00:51:03,433 --> 00:51:06,967
- [Mike] Oh yeah, I knew
there had to be one of those.

1363
00:51:06,967 --> 00:51:09,533
- [Tom] Whacka, whacka, whacka.

1364
00:51:09,533 --> 00:51:11,267
- What's going on
in there, Johnny?

1365
00:51:12,467 --> 00:51:14,067
- Are you game to
try and find out?

1366
00:51:14,933 --> 00:51:16,133
- You don't mean.

1367
00:51:16,133 --> 00:51:17,633
- [Crow] Would you
finish your sentences!

1368
00:51:17,633 --> 00:51:20,000
- The answers to all our
questions lie behind that wall.

1369
00:51:21,933 --> 00:51:24,433
- [Mike] He shot the bear
in mid standing ovation.

1370
00:51:26,767 --> 00:51:29,133
- [Tom] I shouldn't let
Brian Epstein dress me.

1371
00:51:30,533 --> 00:51:32,533
- [Mike] The lion's
looking at me, Hartley.

1372
00:51:36,433 --> 00:51:37,767
- [Crow] Well you
don't think that.

1373
00:51:37,767 --> 00:51:38,700
You're not saying?

1374
00:51:38,700 --> 00:51:39,933
It's not true that.

1375
00:51:39,933 --> 00:51:42,700
- We're gonna find
out right now.

1376
00:51:44,200 --> 00:51:45,033
- [Tom] You know this
room could use more

1377
00:51:45,033 --> 00:51:46,467
of a hunting motif.

1378
00:51:46,467 --> 00:51:48,567
A gamey feel.

1379
00:51:48,567 --> 00:51:51,033
- [Tom] I wanna go see the
hall of record muskies.

1380
00:51:53,567 --> 00:51:54,733
- [Mike] Look at that up there,

1381
00:51:54,733 --> 00:51:56,833
it's a coelacanth on the wall.

1382
00:51:56,833 --> 00:51:58,967
(heavy breathing)

1383
00:51:58,967 --> 00:52:01,100
(dinging)

1384
00:52:02,600 --> 00:52:05,067
- [Crow] Please bear
with me folks, ha, ha.

1385
00:52:05,067 --> 00:52:07,900
Hey did you know in college
I was an Ursa Major.

1386
00:52:07,900 --> 00:52:09,367
I'm kidding!

1387
00:52:09,367 --> 00:52:12,033
(ominous music)

1388
00:52:13,067 --> 00:52:15,267
- [Balleau] Good evening.

1389
00:52:15,267 --> 00:52:17,967
- [Tom] Welcome to my
production of True.

1390
00:52:17,967 --> 00:52:20,333
- This is a pleasant surprise.

1391
00:52:20,333 --> 00:52:21,367
I wasn't expecting you

1392
00:52:21,367 --> 00:52:22,767
but I'm glad to see you.

1393
00:52:23,900 --> 00:52:25,700
Come in, come in won't you?

1394
00:52:25,700 --> 00:52:27,433
- [Tom] Don't step in the guano.

1395
00:52:27,433 --> 00:52:29,767
(squishing)

1396
00:52:31,000 --> 00:52:33,500
- I'd have something
I'd like to show you.

1397
00:52:33,500 --> 00:52:35,667
- [Tom] My mermaid outfit.

1398
00:52:37,167 --> 00:52:38,633
(screaming)

1399
00:52:38,633 --> 00:52:40,233
- [Crow] Mommy, mommy,
I'd walk a million miles.

1400
00:52:40,233 --> 00:52:42,700
- Welcome to my
private trophy room.

1401
00:52:44,233 --> 00:52:46,233
I had intended to
show it to you in time

1402
00:52:46,233 --> 00:52:47,800
but why not now?

1403
00:52:47,800 --> 00:52:49,533
- [Tom] During my
Montclare moment.

1404
00:52:52,300 --> 00:52:54,000
- [Crow] Now that one I like.

1405
00:52:54,000 --> 00:52:55,000
- [Below] That's Jacoby.

1406
00:52:55,000 --> 00:52:56,067
- [Tom] An eyeful.

1407
00:52:56,067 --> 00:52:57,333
He had strangled seven women

1408
00:52:57,333 --> 00:52:58,533
before he found his
way to my island.

1409
00:52:58,533 --> 00:53:00,133
- [Mike] We were
roommates for awhile.

1410
00:53:00,133 --> 00:53:04,400
- His experience made him an
adversary worthy of my skills.

1411
00:53:05,533 --> 00:53:07,900
- You mean you hunted
these people down?

1412
00:53:07,900 --> 00:53:09,433
And then killed them
and did that to them?

1413
00:53:09,433 --> 00:53:10,600
- [Crow] And
finished your degree?

1414
00:53:10,600 --> 00:53:12,100
- Exactly.

1415
00:53:12,100 --> 00:53:16,000
I hunted them and
shall I tell you why?

1416
00:53:16,000 --> 00:53:17,233
- [Tom] Oh cripes, yeah sure.

1417
00:53:17,233 --> 00:53:19,100
- I've been a
scholar all my life.

1418
00:53:19,100 --> 00:53:20,633
- [Mike] I've got a GED.

1419
00:53:20,633 --> 00:53:24,000
- I was curator of a large
museum when the war began.

1420
00:53:24,000 --> 00:53:25,433
- [Crow] The Roger Maris Museum.

1421
00:53:25,433 --> 00:53:27,333
- I, who had never killed
anything in my life,

1422
00:53:27,333 --> 00:53:29,867
found myself assigned
to duty as a sniper.

1423
00:53:29,867 --> 00:53:31,267
- [Tom] Hunker down.

1424
00:53:31,267 --> 00:53:33,000
- A sharp shooter because
of my steady hands

1425
00:53:33,000 --> 00:53:34,167
and keen eyesight.

1426
00:53:34,167 --> 00:53:36,533
- [Tom] 50 monologues
for male actors.

1427
00:53:36,533 --> 00:53:40,633
- It amuses me now that I
found it distasteful, at first.

1428
00:53:40,633 --> 00:53:42,100
- [Mike] Or is it
distasteful that I found out.

1429
00:53:42,100 --> 00:53:45,900
- As time went by I
adjusted to my new activity.

1430
00:53:45,900 --> 00:53:47,333
- [Crow] Uh, sir.

1431
00:53:47,333 --> 00:53:50,633
- What had been an unpleasant
duty became a pleasure.

1432
00:53:50,633 --> 00:53:52,467
Then it developed into a passion

1433
00:53:52,467 --> 00:53:53,733
and then into a lust.

1434
00:53:53,733 --> 00:53:54,700
- [Tom] And then
back into a pleasure.

1435
00:53:54,700 --> 00:53:55,900
- A lust for blood.

1436
00:53:55,900 --> 00:53:59,000
A lust that has
grown with the years

1437
00:53:59,000 --> 00:54:03,467
and one that I spend my entire
life with trying to satisfy.

1438
00:54:03,467 --> 00:54:05,267
- [Mike] Yeah, I like model
building a whole lot too.

1439
00:54:05,267 --> 00:54:07,067
- Don't be afraid.

1440
00:54:07,067 --> 00:54:09,467
I've merely preserved
my trophies as they were

1441
00:54:09,467 --> 00:54:10,900
at the moment of my triumph.

1442
00:54:10,900 --> 00:54:13,933
- [Crow] I wonder if he's
considered commemorative plates.

1443
00:54:13,933 --> 00:54:15,767
(girl screaming)

1444
00:54:15,767 --> 00:54:17,900
- [Tom] Okay, that pose
coulda been a lot worse.

1445
00:54:20,167 --> 00:54:24,433
- I see that my latest trophy
has really impressed you.

1446
00:54:24,433 --> 00:54:27,533
I'm glad, because I think
it's the best thing I've done.

1447
00:54:28,500 --> 00:54:29,667
So far.

1448
00:54:29,667 --> 00:54:30,933
- [Tom] And so
does Hilton Kramer.

1449
00:54:30,933 --> 00:54:32,433
- And because they
tried to betray me

1450
00:54:32,433 --> 00:54:34,433
it gave me great satisfaction.

1451
00:54:34,433 --> 00:54:38,167
- [Crow] Now, I think Salty
Ron has pecan ice cream for us.

1452
00:54:38,167 --> 00:54:39,333
- [Tom] Salty Ron.

1453
00:54:39,333 --> 00:54:40,900
- I must leave you now.

1454
00:54:40,900 --> 00:54:42,300
- [Mike] Am I being fay enough?

1455
00:54:42,300 --> 00:54:43,767
- Since you had no difficulty
finding your way here

1456
00:54:43,767 --> 00:54:46,767
I'm sure that when Miss Scott
has regained her composure

1457
00:54:46,767 --> 00:54:48,667
you'll be able to
see her to her room.

1458
00:54:50,033 --> 00:54:51,733
Oh yes.
- [Tom] I have another cliche.

1459
00:54:51,733 --> 00:54:52,933
- Just one thing more I'd like

1460
00:54:52,933 --> 00:54:54,400
to have you see before you go.

1461
00:54:54,400 --> 00:54:56,000
- [Mike] It's called Captain EO.

1462
00:54:57,267 --> 00:54:58,767
- [Crow] Exhibit under repair.

1463
00:55:00,733 --> 00:55:03,267
- You'll notice that this
niche has room for three.

1464
00:55:04,233 --> 00:55:05,367
Or even four.

1465
00:55:05,367 --> 00:55:06,833
- [Tom] If you
squeeze 'em a little.

1466
00:55:06,833 --> 00:55:08,300
- Trophies.

1467
00:55:08,300 --> 00:55:10,300
- [Tom] It's a little pricey,
but I think it's worth it.

1468
00:55:12,867 --> 00:55:13,700
- Tony!

1469
00:55:15,200 --> 00:55:17,367
- [Crow] Is this
your drunk, Hartley?

1470
00:55:17,367 --> 00:55:19,567
- Yes, Tony.

1471
00:55:19,567 --> 00:55:20,833
- [Mike] Tony, Tony, Tony, Tony.

1472
00:55:20,833 --> 00:55:22,067
- I think it only fair
that we should be here

1473
00:55:22,067 --> 00:55:24,433
at this time because
he's going to take part

1474
00:55:24,433 --> 00:55:25,967
in the game, too.

1475
00:55:25,967 --> 00:55:30,567
- No, no, please don't send
me out there with them.

1476
00:55:30,567 --> 00:55:32,200
- [Tom] Oh, I can't
stay cool anymore.

1477
00:55:32,200 --> 00:55:34,067
This is gonna be so fun!

1478
00:55:35,500 --> 00:55:38,133
- I paid you well to provide
me with subjects in the past.

1479
00:55:38,133 --> 00:55:40,500
I've warned you not to use
your boat for other purposes.

1480
00:55:40,500 --> 00:55:42,833
- [Crow] You went inner
tubing, didn't you?

1481
00:55:42,833 --> 00:55:44,700
- Allow these young people
to find their way here

1482
00:55:44,700 --> 00:55:46,900
without my knowledge,
I think it only right

1483
00:55:46,900 --> 00:55:49,200
that you should
share their fate.

1484
00:55:49,200 --> 00:55:50,200
- Do you mean?

1485
00:55:50,200 --> 00:55:51,133
- [Mike] Stop it!

1486
00:55:51,133 --> 00:55:52,900
- That's exactly what I mean.

1487
00:55:52,900 --> 00:55:55,467
Tony was my source of
supply for subjects.

1488
00:55:56,633 --> 00:55:58,467
In the past they were
always convicts escaped

1489
00:55:58,467 --> 00:55:59,400
from the penal island.

1490
00:55:59,400 --> 00:56:00,567
- [Tom] Te-hee, te-hee.

1491
00:56:00,567 --> 00:56:01,800
- [Crow] Stop it.

1492
00:56:01,800 --> 00:56:03,300
- I find him no
longer dependable.

1493
00:56:03,300 --> 00:56:05,733
- You really think you can
get away with this madness?

1494
00:56:05,733 --> 00:56:07,167
- Madness?

1495
00:56:07,167 --> 00:56:10,000
What I plan is a pleasant
evening's diversion.

1496
00:56:10,000 --> 00:56:12,967
- See I knew he was only
kidding. Well that's just a toy.

1497
00:56:12,967 --> 00:56:14,433
That couldn't kill anyone.

1498
00:56:14,433 --> 00:56:16,733
- [Tom] You're forgetting about
the boiled heads, aren't ya?

1499
00:56:16,733 --> 00:56:18,567
- [Mike] Is that Alf
on the wall back there?

1500
00:56:18,567 --> 00:56:20,067
- [Tom] I hope so.

1501
00:56:21,600 --> 00:56:23,733
- [Crow] Somebody took my bones.

1502
00:56:25,867 --> 00:56:27,800
- [Tom] It amuses me
and is also distasteful

1503
00:56:27,800 --> 00:56:29,633
that you think that I would.

1504
00:56:30,900 --> 00:56:32,600
(shattering)

1505
00:56:32,600 --> 00:56:33,933
- [Mike] Did I hit him, or?

1506
00:56:35,567 --> 00:56:36,667
- [Crow] Huh.

1507
00:56:38,100 --> 00:56:40,433
- [Tom] Well, that threw
cold water on everything.

1508
00:56:40,433 --> 00:56:42,900
- I guess it could
kill somebody.

1509
00:56:44,000 --> 00:56:45,233
- Yes, it can.

1510
00:56:45,233 --> 00:56:46,600
- [Mike] Now go
change your pants.

1511
00:56:46,600 --> 00:56:48,600
- And soon we'll
try to prove it.

1512
00:56:48,600 --> 00:56:51,233
- Please Mr. Balleau,
don't do this awful thing.

1513
00:56:51,233 --> 00:56:52,733
- Oh please.

1514
00:56:52,733 --> 00:56:54,233
- You have nothing to be
alarmed about young lady.

1515
00:56:54,233 --> 00:56:57,067
You're in no danger,
either of you.

1516
00:56:57,067 --> 00:56:59,433
Your life here with me
will be a pleasant one.

1517
00:56:59,433 --> 00:57:02,267
You'll find me a most
accommodating host.

1518
00:57:02,267 --> 00:57:03,800
- Do you think Jeanne
and I will have anything

1519
00:57:03,800 --> 00:57:06,067
to do with you if you
sent Pete and Johnny out

1520
00:57:06,067 --> 00:57:07,633
and kill them in that jungle?

1521
00:57:07,633 --> 00:57:08,867
- Yes, I think so.

1522
00:57:08,867 --> 00:57:10,333
I know so.

1523
00:57:10,333 --> 00:57:13,600
No one has ever left this
island, dead or alive.

1524
00:57:13,600 --> 00:57:15,600
And since the unfortunate
incident involving

1525
00:57:15,600 --> 00:57:18,333
my wife has left me
without feminine company.

1526
00:57:18,333 --> 00:57:19,833
- [Crow] Except
for Minnie Pearl.

1527
00:57:19,833 --> 00:57:22,867
- Getting to know you
both better, much better.

1528
00:57:22,867 --> 00:57:24,800
- [Tom] Boy he bounces back
from a bad relationship.

1529
00:57:24,800 --> 00:57:26,267
- So it's not enough that you
have to make sitting ducks

1530
00:57:26,267 --> 00:57:29,733
out of Pete and me, you
have to hurt the girls too?

1531
00:57:29,733 --> 00:57:31,067
- Hurt them?

1532
00:57:31,067 --> 00:57:32,933
- Johnny, he really means it.

1533
00:57:32,933 --> 00:57:33,967
What are we gonna do?

1534
00:57:35,600 --> 00:57:36,767
- [Tom] Oh, how'd
that get there?

1535
00:57:36,767 --> 00:57:37,700
- I think you've
asked the wrong person

1536
00:57:37,700 --> 00:57:39,000
about your future activity.

1537
00:57:40,333 --> 00:57:41,367
Go with Jondor.

1538
00:57:42,433 --> 00:57:43,767
Jondor lock them in their room

1539
00:57:43,767 --> 00:57:45,100
and then come back at once.

1540
00:57:47,100 --> 00:57:48,700
- [Mike] His real
name's Clint but Jondor

1541
00:57:48,700 --> 00:57:50,333
has a certain ring.

1542
00:57:50,333 --> 00:57:51,733
- [Crow] It does.

1543
00:57:52,833 --> 00:57:55,633
Well, he's kinda cute, I guess.

1544
00:57:55,633 --> 00:57:57,467
I can change him.

1545
00:57:57,467 --> 00:58:00,167
(ominous music)

1546
00:58:02,200 --> 00:58:03,633
- [Mike] All right, mad men.

1547
00:58:03,633 --> 00:58:06,700
- Very touching, but to
return to your question, Pete.

1548
00:58:06,700 --> 00:58:08,000
- [Crow] The Mugwumps way.

1549
00:58:09,467 --> 00:58:11,500
- Up to a certain point. The
rest then will be up to you.

1550
00:58:12,467 --> 00:58:14,033
Are you listening to this, Tony?

1551
00:58:14,033 --> 00:58:14,900
It applies to you, too.

1552
00:58:14,900 --> 00:58:16,600
- [Mike] Yup, okay.

1553
00:58:16,600 --> 00:58:17,800
- Moonlight's bright
tonight so you will make

1554
00:58:17,800 --> 00:58:20,167
your way to the starting
point of the chase.

1555
00:58:20,167 --> 00:58:21,700
It's in the center
of the jungle.

1556
00:58:21,700 --> 00:58:23,533
Ivor will give you
exact directions.

1557
00:58:23,533 --> 00:58:25,000
- [Tom] Eyeball?

1558
00:58:25,000 --> 00:58:26,800
- The natives used to
call it the Tree of Death.

1559
00:58:26,800 --> 00:58:28,267
- [Crow] I call it Bernie.

1560
00:58:28,267 --> 00:58:29,800
- I will give you plenty
of time to plan your flight

1561
00:58:29,800 --> 00:58:34,800
before I follow with the cross
bow and just three of these.

1562
00:58:35,933 --> 00:58:38,200
Just three shots, that's
all I allow myself.

1563
00:58:38,200 --> 00:58:39,567
- [Tom] Or else I get silly.

1564
00:58:39,567 --> 00:58:40,633
- That's sporting
don't you think?

1565
00:58:41,533 --> 00:58:42,467
- [Crow] Ah, Eyeball.

1566
00:58:42,467 --> 00:58:44,000
- [Tom] It's Jondor, sir.

1567
00:58:44,000 --> 00:58:45,267
- You haven't answered me.

1568
00:58:45,267 --> 00:58:46,600
Don't you think it's
very sporting of me

1569
00:58:46,600 --> 00:58:49,133
to allow myself just one
chance at each of you?

1570
00:58:50,033 --> 00:58:52,300
- Oh sure, really sporting.

1571
00:58:52,300 --> 00:58:53,333
- [Tom] Frankly, I'm steamed.

1572
00:58:53,333 --> 00:58:55,233
- Marcus of Queensbury.

1573
00:58:55,233 --> 00:58:57,900
- And a jungle like
the palm of your hand.

1574
00:58:57,900 --> 00:58:59,767
With that thing that could
knock an elephant over.

1575
00:58:59,767 --> 00:59:01,767
You're gonna hunt us
down but we have nothing

1576
00:59:01,767 --> 00:59:03,033
to defend ourselves with.

1577
00:59:03,033 --> 00:59:05,467
- Not so fast, not so fast.

1578
00:59:05,467 --> 00:59:06,700
You haven't allowed me to finish

1579
00:59:06,700 --> 00:59:08,867
the entire plan of the
evening's entertainment.

1580
00:59:08,867 --> 00:59:10,633
- [Crow] There will
be a light deck lunch.

1581
00:59:10,633 --> 00:59:12,900
- Tony, here catch.

1582
00:59:12,900 --> 00:59:15,300
- [Mike] Where do you
drink outta this thing?

1583
00:59:15,300 --> 00:59:18,367
- Oh Tony, it isn't loaded.

1584
00:59:18,367 --> 00:59:20,633
- [Crow] Neither am I,
oh please just a taste.

1585
00:59:20,633 --> 00:59:22,967
- He had us alone in here
with an unloaded gun.

1586
00:59:24,267 --> 00:59:25,533
- It's time to start.

1587
00:59:25,533 --> 00:59:28,167
The odds are not
completely one sided.

1588
00:59:28,167 --> 00:59:31,033
Your youth and numbers against
my skill and experience.

1589
00:59:32,267 --> 00:59:33,533
You'll find the ammunition

1590
00:59:33,533 --> 00:59:35,933
in the branches of
the tree of death.

1591
00:59:35,933 --> 00:59:39,667
When that gun is loaded, my
life will also be subject

1592
00:59:39,667 --> 00:59:41,167
to how the hunt goes.

1593
00:59:41,167 --> 00:59:43,167
- [Tom] Tonight on
the Hollywood Palace.

1594
00:59:45,667 --> 00:59:47,433
- [Mike] Oh, oh real good plan.

1595
00:59:47,433 --> 00:59:50,700
Let's go to the Tree
of Death right away.

1596
00:59:54,033 --> 00:59:55,267
Maybe there's a map
that tells us the way

1597
00:59:55,267 --> 00:59:56,500
to the Tree of Death.

1598
00:59:56,500 --> 00:59:59,500
- [Crow] Let's visit
Mr. Owl, hoo hoo.

1599
00:59:59,500 --> 01:00:01,867
- [Mike] Tree of novelty items.

1600
01:00:01,867 --> 01:00:04,433
- Here it is, I found it.
- [Crow] It's Meow Meow Kitty.

1601
01:00:04,433 --> 01:00:05,267
Meow, meow, meow.

1602
01:00:08,233 --> 01:00:09,267
- Why that rat.

1603
01:00:10,333 --> 01:00:12,800
That lousy stinking rat.

1604
01:00:12,800 --> 01:00:14,033
- [Tom] Let's not call names

1605
01:00:14,033 --> 01:00:15,733
it'll escalate in to bitterness.

1606
01:00:15,733 --> 01:00:17,600
- He left only one shell.

1607
01:00:17,600 --> 01:00:19,667
- Because that's way
his twisted mind works.

1608
01:00:19,667 --> 01:00:20,733
- [Tom] I don't
like the villain.

1609
01:00:20,733 --> 01:00:21,567
- What'd ya mean?

1610
01:00:21,567 --> 01:00:23,067
- Don't ya see?

1611
01:00:23,067 --> 01:00:24,833
He allows himself three
shots, one for each of us.

1612
01:00:24,833 --> 01:00:26,533
And we get one shot at him.

1613
01:00:26,533 --> 01:00:28,667
- [Mike] I'm just about fed up.

1614
01:00:28,667 --> 01:00:30,167
- Well there's nothing
we can do about it.

1615
01:00:30,167 --> 01:00:31,767
Come on, give me the gun
and let's get moving.

1616
01:00:31,767 --> 01:00:33,400
- Oh no you don't, buster.

1617
01:00:33,400 --> 01:00:35,167
You just hand over that bullet.

1618
01:00:35,167 --> 01:00:36,100
- Don't be a fool, Tony.

1619
01:00:36,100 --> 01:00:37,867
I'm a better shot than you are.

1620
01:00:37,867 --> 01:00:39,100
- Yeah?

1621
01:00:39,100 --> 01:00:40,633
- [Tom] Are you
saying I'm a drunk?

1622
01:00:40,633 --> 01:00:41,833
- Have you ever shot a man?

1623
01:00:41,833 --> 01:00:44,167
- [Crow] Well I slapped
Andy Davis once.

1624
01:00:44,167 --> 01:00:45,433
- No.

1625
01:00:45,433 --> 01:00:46,733
- Well I have.

1626
01:00:46,733 --> 01:00:49,333
And I ain't taking any
chances no amateur.

1627
01:00:51,733 --> 01:00:53,367
- [Crow] (imitates
gunshot) whoops.

1628
01:00:55,133 --> 01:00:56,133
Are you done?

1629
01:00:56,133 --> 01:00:57,600
- Well which way should we go?

1630
01:00:58,800 --> 01:01:00,733
- I don't know which way
you two guys are going

1631
01:01:00,733 --> 01:01:02,400
but I'm going alone.

1632
01:01:03,267 --> 01:01:05,100
So long, suckers.

1633
01:01:05,100 --> 01:01:06,267
- Hey.

1634
01:01:06,267 --> 01:01:07,600
- [Tom] Well now
that was really rude.

1635
01:01:07,600 --> 01:01:10,667
- Hey come back here!
- No, no let him go.

1636
01:01:10,667 --> 01:01:11,933
- [Crow] I liked him.

1637
01:01:11,933 --> 01:01:12,767
- It's better if we do split up.

1638
01:01:12,767 --> 01:01:13,700
It doubles our chances.

1639
01:01:13,700 --> 01:01:15,233
- How?

1640
01:01:15,233 --> 01:01:16,667
- Well Balleau can't hunt
in two directions at once.

1641
01:01:16,667 --> 01:01:18,067
Now look, the way I figure
it, we must be right

1642
01:01:18,067 --> 01:01:19,567
about in the middle
of the island.

1643
01:01:19,567 --> 01:01:22,100
If we were to head that way,
try to get to the beach,

1644
01:01:22,100 --> 01:01:24,267
and then work our way
back to the house.

1645
01:01:24,267 --> 01:01:25,933
- Back to the
house, are ya nuts?

1646
01:01:25,933 --> 01:01:27,767
- [Mike] Well, I've got
a slight dementia but.

1647
01:01:27,767 --> 01:01:29,267
- Well let's try
to find the boat.

1648
01:01:29,267 --> 01:01:30,733
- No, we've gotta try and
get the girls out first.

1649
01:01:30,733 --> 01:01:32,200
- But Balleau said
he won't touch them.

1650
01:01:32,200 --> 01:01:33,433
- Never mind what he said,
I know what he meant.

1651
01:01:33,433 --> 01:01:35,167
- [Crow] Filthy
un-Christian sex.

1652
01:01:36,367 --> 01:01:38,133
- Come on, let's get started.

1653
01:01:38,133 --> 01:01:39,967
- I just don't see
how you figure--

1654
01:01:39,967 --> 01:01:42,633
- Nevermind, we've
gotta do what we can

1655
01:01:42,633 --> 01:01:44,167
and that's the first thing.

1656
01:01:44,167 --> 01:01:46,433
- [Mike] They found the tree
of interminable dialogue.

1657
01:01:48,333 --> 01:01:50,567
- Well here's hoping we
find the second thing

1658
01:01:50,567 --> 01:01:51,400
whatever that'll be.

1659
01:01:51,400 --> 01:01:52,467
- [Tom] I heard that.

1660
01:01:53,800 --> 01:01:55,533
- [Mike] I'm almost
ready mad doctor, dear.

1661
01:01:55,533 --> 01:01:56,667
Just freshening up.

1662
01:01:56,667 --> 01:01:58,067
- The guard's still out there

1663
01:01:58,067 --> 01:01:59,400
and wide awake.

1664
01:01:59,400 --> 01:02:00,400
- [Crow] And cute.

1665
01:02:00,400 --> 01:02:01,633
- Betty what are we gonna do?

1666
01:02:01,633 --> 01:02:03,467
Even if we do get
outta this room

1667
01:02:03,467 --> 01:02:04,833
where are we gonna go?

1668
01:02:04,833 --> 01:02:06,000
- I don't know.

1669
01:02:06,000 --> 01:02:07,133
- [Tom] We could
have a slumber party.

1670
01:02:07,133 --> 01:02:08,400
- Maybe we could find the boys

1671
01:02:08,400 --> 01:02:10,200
and try to get to the boat.

1672
01:02:10,200 --> 01:02:11,867
- We don't even
know where it is.

1673
01:02:12,867 --> 01:02:14,367
Oh, it's just no use.

1674
01:02:15,267 --> 01:02:20,167
♪ Where is love♪

1675
01:02:20,167 --> 01:02:22,200
- [Mike] Am I thinking
what I'm thinking?

1676
01:02:24,833 --> 01:02:26,767
- [Crow] Use your weighty hulk.

1677
01:02:26,767 --> 01:02:28,400
- Oh, even this
window is locked.

1678
01:02:32,033 --> 01:02:34,200
- [Crow] Oh, even the
spittoon is locked.

1679
01:02:34,200 --> 01:02:36,267
I could make placements
for the children.

1680
01:02:38,900 --> 01:02:40,300
- Come here, Jeanne.

1681
01:02:40,300 --> 01:02:42,467
- [Tom] Come on, move
it, move it, move it!

1682
01:02:43,367 --> 01:02:46,267
- Listen to me, I got an idea.

1683
01:02:46,267 --> 01:02:48,967
Don't ask any questions,
just do exactly as I say.

1684
01:02:50,133 --> 01:02:51,900
- All right, what do
you want me to do.

1685
01:02:51,900 --> 01:02:52,700
- Here.
- Oh.

1686
01:02:53,900 --> 01:02:55,467
- [Tom] I'm blind Tom, yar.

1687
01:02:56,533 --> 01:02:57,500
(shattering)

1688
01:02:57,500 --> 01:02:58,933
- [Crow] Just set that anywhere.

1689
01:03:02,400 --> 01:03:04,967
- Oh, I'm terribly sorry.

1690
01:03:04,967 --> 01:03:06,533
- [Tom] Not the Ming spittoon.

1691
01:03:06,533 --> 01:03:10,133
- I was moving it
and it just broke.

1692
01:03:10,133 --> 01:03:13,467
I'm sure I can fix it if
you could get me some glue.

1693
01:03:13,467 --> 01:03:15,100
- [Mike] Oh, this is
their cutest plan yet.

1694
01:03:15,100 --> 01:03:16,600
- I didn't mean to break it, it.

1695
01:03:16,600 --> 01:03:17,533
- [Tom] It broke.

1696
01:03:17,533 --> 01:03:18,800
- It was such a pretty vase.

1697
01:03:19,933 --> 01:03:21,633
(cackling)

1698
01:03:21,633 --> 01:03:24,133
- [Crow] Damn you all to hell!

1699
01:03:25,800 --> 01:03:27,967
- [Mike] New stuff killed.

1700
01:03:29,367 --> 01:03:30,767
- Jeanne come on, hurry.

1701
01:03:30,767 --> 01:03:32,433
- [Tom] But we need
to fix the vase.

1702
01:03:32,433 --> 01:03:34,100
- Come on, I'll
help you climb out.

1703
01:03:34,100 --> 01:03:35,267
- Out there?

1704
01:03:35,267 --> 01:03:36,300
But we'll be killed.

1705
01:03:36,300 --> 01:03:37,233
- No we won't.

1706
01:03:37,233 --> 01:03:38,733
There's a ledge out there.

1707
01:03:38,733 --> 01:03:40,867
We can work along that until
we come to an open window.

1708
01:03:40,867 --> 01:03:41,800
- What if there's something--

1709
01:03:41,800 --> 01:03:43,133
- Never mind, it's the only way.

1710
01:03:43,133 --> 01:03:44,833
Come on, hurry up.

1711
01:03:44,833 --> 01:03:45,900
Be careful, too.

1712
01:03:45,900 --> 01:03:47,700
- [Crow] Now, fly monkey's fly.

1713
01:03:47,700 --> 01:03:49,867
(humming)

1714
01:03:51,333 --> 01:03:53,833
(light music)

1715
01:03:57,667 --> 01:03:59,000
- [Crow] Action foliage.

